    "ls|LS17_S14_U_1": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF REVENUE \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *1 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON MONDAY, 04th DECEMBER, 2023 / \nAGRAHAYANA 13, 1945 (SAKA)  \n \nPURCHASE OF HOUSING UNITS BY CENTRAL EXCISE DEPARTMENT \n*1. SHRI KANAKMAL KATARA: \nWill the Minister of Finance be pleased to state: \na) whether the Central Excise Department has purchased dwelling Units from \nRajasthan Housing Board in Jaipur for their officers and employees; \n \nb) if so, the details thereof indicating the number of such housing units purchased \nand the amount of funds spent thereon; \n \nc) the number of such housing units currently lying vacant along with the reasons \ntherefor; \n \nd) whether any written complaint have been received by the Government \nregarding the dilapidated conditions of the  said housing units and conduct of \nanti-social activities in such houses lying vacant; and \n \ne) if so, the details thereof and the action taken or likely to be taken by the \nGovernment on such complaints? \n \nANSWER \nFINANCE MINISTER  \n(SMT NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *1 FOR ANSWER ON 04.12.2023 RAISED BY SHRI KANAKMAL \nKATARA, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING “PURCHASE OF \nHOUSING UNITS BY CENTRAL EXCISE DEPARTMENT”. \n(a) Yes. \n(b) A total of 12 dwelling units were purchased at Jaipur for an amount of Rs. 1.02 \ncrore.  \n(c) One (1) dwelling unit out of 12 units is vacant at present as no one has applied. \n(d)  No complaint has been received about these dwelling units in Jaipur.  \n(e) Question does not arise in view of (d) above. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_10": "58,501 women beneficiaries have been assisted under others schemes during the last \nthree years including current year upto 28.11.2023. \n \n�������� The following are the eligibility criteria for availing financial assistance \nfrom NSTFDC including tribal women:  \n \n�� All applicant(s)/ member(s) should belong to the Scheduled Tribes Community. \n�� Annual family income of the applicant(s) should not exceed ₹3.00 lakh p.a. \nboth for rural and urban areas.  \n \n�������� From time to time, NSTFDC in collaboration with the implementing agencies \nconduct awareness camps for disseminating information about its schemes. Further, \nthe Corporation also participate in the events/ programmes of other organisations/ \ndepartments. \n������������",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_100": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CHEMICALS & FERTILIZERS \nDEPARTMENT OF PHARMACEUTICALS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 100 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 08th DECEMBER, 2023 \n  \nPromotion of Medical Device Industry  \n \n \n*100.  SHRIMATI SUMALATHA AMBAREESH:  \nSHRI D.K. SURESH:  \n \nWill the Minister of CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) the present status of growth of Medical Device Industry in the country;  \n(b) the details of the initiatives taken/proposed to be taken by the Government for promotion \nof Medical Device Industry;  \n(c) whether the Government has identified potential hubs in various States for the \ndevelopment of Medical Device Industry in the country; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken thereon? \n  \n \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n (Dr. MANSUKH MANDAVIYA)  \n \n(a)  to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n \n \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to the LOK SABHA STARRED Q. NO. 100 (20 th \nPOSITION) for answer on 08.12.2023 raised by SHRIMATI SUMALATHA \nAMBAREESH and SHRI D.K. SURESH regarding “Promotion of Medical Device \nIndustry” \n  \n(a): It is informed that the market size of medical device industry in the country was estimated \nat USD 11 billion in 2020 and is expected to grow rapidly. India is the 4th largest Asian medical \ndevices market after Japan, China and South Korea and among t he top 20 global medical \ndevices markets in the world. \n \n(b): The Government of India has taken several measures to encourage domestic manufacturing \nof Medical Devices. The steps taken to support Medical Devices Industries are as under: \n  \ni. Production Linked Incentive Scheme for Promoting Domestic Manufacturing of \nMedical Devices (PLI MD) with total financial outlay of Rs. 3,420 crore and tenure \nfrom FY 2020-2021 to FY 2027 -28. The financial incentive is to be given to selected \ncompanies at the rate of 5% on incremental sales of medical devices manufactured in \nIndia and covered under the target segments of the scheme, for a period of five years. \n26 participants have been approved under the scheme and manufacturing of 39 products \nhas started.  \nii. Production Linked Incentive Scheme for Pharmaceuticals has been launched with total \nfinancial outlay of Rs. 15,000 crore and tenure from FY 2020-2021 to FY 2028-29. The \nscheme covers In-Vitro Diagnostic (IVD) Device. A total of 55 participants have been \napproved under the scheme, out of which 5 participants were selected for In -Vitro \nDiagnostic (IVD) devices.   \niii. The scheme Promotion of Medical Device s Parks, with a total financial outlay of Rs. \n400 crore and tenure from FY 2020-2021 to FY 2024-2025, provides for the maximum \nfinancial assistance of Rs. 100 crore each to four selected States/Union Territories for \ncreation of Common Infrastructure Facilities in the upcoming Medical Devices Parks. \nUnder the scheme, final approval for fin ancial assistance of Rs. 100 crore each, has \nbeen given to the States of Himachal Pradesh, Madhya Pradesh, Tamil Nadu and Uttar \nPradesh. First installment of Rs 30 crore each has been released to all the States. \niv. The Department has also notified National Me dical Devices Po licy (NMD), 2023 on \n26.04.2023. Policy envisions to place the Indian medical devices sector on an \naccelerated growth path with a patient-centric approach to meet the evolving healthcare \nneeds of the country.  \nv. Scheme for Promotion of Research and Innovation in Pharma MedTech Sector (PRIP) \nin specified priority areas with an outlay of Rs. 5000 cr. for a period of 5 years i.e., \n2023-24 to 2027-28 was notified on 17.08.2023. Under this scheme, i t is proposed to \nestablish a Centre of Excellence (CoE ) with specialization in Medical Devices  at \nNational Institutes of Pharmaceutical Education & Research (NIPER), Ahmedabad. \n  \n(c) & (d) : As per the study conducted through Centre for Market Research & Social \nDevelopment, th ere are 21 Medical Devic e (MD) Clusters in the country. The \nscheme Assistance to Medical Device Clusters for Common Facilities (AMD-CF) provides for \nfinancial incentive to Medical devices clusters to develop common infrastructure facilities like \nMedical devices testing labs, E-waste treatment facility, logistic centers etc. The scheme also \nprovides for financial assistance to National or State Level Government or Private Institutions \ninterested in establishing or strengthening testing facilities for medical  devices. The financial \noutlay of the Scheme is Rs 300 cr and the tenure of the scheme is FY 2023-24 to FY 2026-27.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_101": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *101 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nRetail Inflation \n*101. SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR RINKU: \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has taken note that the country’s retail inflation is increasing \nsteeply; \n(b) if so, the details of the inflation at the current rate; \n(c) whether the Government has conducted any study to find out the reasons for the increase in \ninflation;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to bring down the level of inflation in the \ncountry? \nANSWER \n \nTHE FINANCE MINISTER \n(SMT NIRMALA SITHARAMAN) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 101 RAISED BY SHRI SUSHIL KUMAR RINKU  FOR 11 th \nDECEMBER, 2023 ON “RETAIL INFLATION” \n(a) & (b): India’s retail inflation has declined from an average of 7.1 per cent in April-October \n2022 to 5.4 per cent in the corresponding period of 2023. The retail inflation is now stable and \nwithin the notified tolerance band of 2 per cent to 6 per cent. A steady decline in core inflation, \nestimated after removing volatile food and fuel items from retail inflation , has been critical in \nweakening the inflationary pressure in the Indian economy. The core inflation has declined \nfrom 5.1 per cent in April 2023 to 4.3 per cent in October 2023.  \n(c) & (d): After the notification of the inflation tolerance band in 2016, retail inflation in India \nhas been mostly within acceptable limits. Temporary increases in inflation on a few occasions  \nare caused by  demand-supply misma tches arising out of global shocks and adverse weather \nconditions. Proactive supply side initiatives by the Government and effective demand \nstabilization measures by the Reserve Bank of India  have helped resolve the demand-supply \nmismatches and to rein in inflation. \n(e): The measures taken by the Government to restrain inflation include strengthening of \nbuffers of key food items and making periodic open market releases , easing imports of \nessential food items through trade policy measures , preventing hoarding through \nimposition/revision of stock limits, and channelling supplies through designated retail outlets. \nTo ensure food security to the poor , the Pradhan Mantri Garib Kalyan Anna Yojana, providing \nfree food grains to about 81.35 crore beneficiaries, has been extended for a period of five years \nwith effect from 1 st January 2024.  In October 2023, Government also increased the subsidy \nunder Pradhan Mantri Ujjwala Yojana from Rs. 200 to Rs.300 per 14.2 kg cylinder. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_102": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 102 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nMIGRANT CHILD LABOUR \n \n102.  DR. SHASHI THAROOR: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n  \n(a)the number of migrant child labour in the country as a whole and \nparticularly in Kerala State during the period 2019-22; and  \n(b)the details of child labour (inter -State and intra-State) rescued in \neach State by the Labour Department during the years 2019-22? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) & (b): The Government has enacted the Child Labour (Prohibition \n& Regulation) Act, 1986 which was amended in 2016.  The amended \nAct is called the Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and \nRegulation)  Act, 1986.  The Act provides for complete prohibition of \nwork or employment of children below 14 years in any occupation and \nprocess and adolescents in the age group of 14 to 18 years in \nhazardous occupations and processes. As per “Crime in India” a \npublication of National Crime Records Bureau, 772, 476 and 613 \nnumber of cases were registered during calendar year  2019,2020 and \n2021 respectively under Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and \nRegulation) Act, 1986 in the country. State-wise details are annexed. \n \n*                                                     ***** \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( a) & (b)  OF LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 102 FOR 04.12 .2023 RAISED BY DR. SHASHI \nTHAROOR REGARDING MIGRANT CHILD LABOUR. \n \nNumber of cases registered under Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and \nRegulation) Act, 1986, State / Union Territories -wise: \nSl State/UT 2019 2020 2021 \n1 Andhra Pradesh 2 37 12 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh 0 1 0 \n3 Assam 68 40 78 \n4 Bihar 15 3 14 \n5 Chhattisgarh 2 0 0 \n6 Gujarat 64 39 40 \n7 Haryana 11 1 12 \n8 Himachal Pradesh 0 1 0 \n9 Jharkhand 18 27 5 \n10 Karnataka 83 54 58 \n11 Kerala 2 0 3 \n12 Madhya Pradesh 4 1 5 \n13 Maharashtra 53 29 57 \n14 Meghalaya 2 0 0 \n15 Odisha 0 0 6 \n16 Punjab 8 11 8 \n17 Rajasthan 48 30 19 \n18 Tamil Nadu 3 2 26 \n19 Telangana 314 147 224 \n20 Tripura 0 1 0 \n21 Uttar Pradesh 9 1 1 \n22 Uttarakhand 27 41 25 \n23 West Bengal 7 3 2 \n24 Chandigarh 0 1 7 \n25 D&N Haveli and Daman \n& Diu+ 2 0 0 \n26 Delhi 30 6 11 \n  TOTAL  772 476 613 \n Source: Crime in India, National Crime Records Bureau*** \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_103": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 103 \nANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nEDUCATION PROJECTS IN JHARKHAND \n \n*103. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH CHOUDHARY: \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \na) the details of educational projects being run in Jharkhand, district-wise; \n(b) the details of funds allocated/released for these projects in Jharkhand during the last four years and \nthe current year; \n(c) the total amount of funds spent on Higher Education projects in Jharkhand during the aforesaid \nperiod, district-wise; and \n(d) the steps taken/being taken by the Government for effective implementation of such educational \nprojects in Jharkhand? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the table of the House \n**** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (D) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 103 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 ASKED BY SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH \nCHOUDHARY, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING EDUCATION PROJECTS \nIN JHARKHAND \n(a) to (c) Under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Rashtriya Uchchatar Shiksha Abhiyan (RUSA), \nfinancial support for various higher education projects is provided to States/UTs including Jharkhand. \nThe district-wise details of projects approved in Jharkhand are Annexed. As against the approved total \ncentral share of Rs. 380.6 crores, since inception of the scheme an amount of Rs. 259.28 crores has been \nreleased out of which an amount of Rs. 103.86 crores has been released to the State since 2019-20 (last \nfour years and current year, till date) . As on 06.12.2023, the State has an unspent balance of Rs. 40.88 \ncrores in its Single Nodal  Agency Account. Further releases shall be subject to utilisation of unspent \nbalance and compliance to the scheme guidelines. \nIn addition , Central government provides fund for other Centrally funded Higher Education \nInstitutions under the Department of Higher Education in the State of Jharkhand and funds released \nduring the last four years and the current year for such institutions are as below: \nName of \nInstitutions \nName of \nDistrict where \nlocated  \nFunds released (in Rs. crores) \n  2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 2023-24 \n(as on date) \nIndian Institute of \nTechnology (Indian \nSchool of Mines) \nDhanbad 308.99 319.77 264.84 316.51 271.05 \nNational Institute of \nTechnology Jamshedpur 94.30 80.19 98.07 120.82 100.20 \nIndian Institute of \nManagement Ranchi 25.00 51.00 116.68 - - \nIndian Institute of \nInformation \nTechnology (PPP) \nRanchi 5.12 6.24 11.51 18.95 25.00 \nCentral University of \nJharkhand Ranchi 51.54 71.80 68.12 93.01 72.80 \nNational Institute of \nAdvanced \nManufacturing \nTechnology \nRanchi 47.37 43.21 47.97 54.53 - \n \n  \n\n(d) Education being in the Concurrent List,  implementation and monitoring of educational projects is \nthe responsibility of both the Central and State Governments.  The Government is monitoring for \neffective implementation of these education projects through regular online/physical review meetings \nwith stakeholders. \n**** \n  \n\nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 103 TO BE ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 ASKED BY SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH \nCHOUDHARY, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING EDUCATION PROJECTS \nIN JHARKHAND \nDistrict-wise details of projects approved in Jharkhand under the scheme of Rashtriya Uchchatar Shiksha \nAbhiyan (RUSA) \nS. No. \n \nDistrict Names Number of \nUnits Approved \nTotal Amount \nApproved (in Rs. crore) \nCentral Share \nApproved (in Rs. \ncrore) \n1.  Bokaro 8 42.0 25.2 \n2.  Chatra 3 18.0 10.8 \n3.  Deoghar 4 8.0 4.8 \n4.  Dhanbad 7 14.0 8.4 \n5.  Dumka 6 42.0 25.2 \n6.  East Singhbhum 9 97.0 58.2 \n7.  Garhwa 2 14.0 8.4 \n8.  Giridih 3 18.0 10.8 \n9.  Godda 4 34.0 20.4 \n10.  Gumla 2 14.0 8.4 \n11.  Hazaribagh 7 36.0 21.6 \n12.  Koderma 2 28.0 16.8 \n13.  Latehar 1 12.0 7.2 \n14.  Lohardaga 1 2.0 1.2 \n15.  Pakur 2 16.0 9.6 \n16.  Palamu 3 32.0 19.2 \n17.  Ramgarh 4 20.0 12.0 \n18.  Ranchi 14 104.0 62.4 \n19.  Sahibganj 4 20.0 12.0 \n20.  Seraikela kharsawan 1 2.0 1.2 \n21.  Simdega 1 12.0 7.2 \n22.  West Singhbhum 4 28.0 16.8 \n23.  State as a Unit 3 17.2 12.8 \n Total 95 630.2 380.6 \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_104": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 104 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nForest Fires  \n \n104. SHRI JAGANNATH SARKAR: \n \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether any steps have been taken to prevent and address forest fires in the country; \n(b)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c)    whether the Government is facing any challenge in implementing its National Forest    \n        Certification Scheme, particularly in addressing the needs and challenges faced by   \n        small-scale producers in developing countries; \n(d)   if so, the details thereof, if not, the reasons therefor; \n(e)  whether the Government has taken any initiatives to ensure the exchange of traditional \nknowledge, technology innovation and community engagement practices in the context \nof forest fire prevention and sustainable forest management; and \n (f)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE \nCHANGE  \n (SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b) The Protection and Management of forest and Wildlife including prevention and \ncontrol of forest fire is primarily the responsibility of concerned State Governments /UT \nAdministrations. The Ministry supports their efforts by providing financial assistance under the \nCentrally Sponsored Forest Fire Prevention and Management Scheme, CAMPA and under \nDevelopment of Wildlife Habitat schemes. These schemes provide support in various activities \nto the state forest departments towards forest fire prevention and mitigation activities including, \ncreation and maintenance of fire lines, water conservation structures, procurement of \nfirefighting equipment, awareness creation etc. \n \nBesides, Forest Survey of India (FSI), generate and disseminates satellite based information, \nwhich is “Weekly Early-Warning Alerts” of forest fire and also “Large Forest Fire Monitoring” \nalerts, to the filed forest officials who are registered with FSI. These fire alerts aim at improving \n\ntactical and strategic response to large forest fires by the respective States/UTs Administrations. \nCurrently the number of registered users is around 2.14 lakh across the country. Further, Van \nAgni Geo Portal is an app developed by FSI monitors large forest fires in various States/UTs \nthroughout the fire season and accordingly alerted the State Forest Departments.  \n  \n (c) & (d) The Ministry has released Indian Forest Management Standard as a part of National \nWorking Plan Code – 2023. This standard is a basis for monitoring of sustainable forest \nmanagement in terms of a broad framework comprising criteria, indicators and verifiers which \nprovides the platform for Indian Forest and Wood Certification Scheme to address the \nchallenges of small scale producers.     \n \n(e) & (f) The Implementation of Forest Fire Prevention and Management Scheme is done \nthrough Joint Forest Management Committee (JFMc) and Village Eco-Development \nCommittees by involving active participation of local Community.  \n \nFurther, this Ministry has formulated the National Action Plan on Forest Fire (NAPFF) in the \nyear 2018 to minimize forest fires by informing, enabling and empowering forest fringe \ncommunities and incentivizing them to work in tandem with the State Forest Departments. The \nMinistry has also constituted a Central Monitoring Committee (CMC) which reviews the \nimplementation of NAPFF and preparedness of States/UTs regarding Forest Fire prevention and \nmanagement in the country. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_105": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 105 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nCENTRAL SECTOR SCHEME FOR REHABILITATION OF BONDED \nLABOURERS IN ODISHA \n \n105.  SHRI BASANTA KUMAR PANDA:  \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate:  \n \n(a)the details of Central Sector Schemes for Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer; \n(b)the number of beneficiaries in Odisha including Kalahandi and \nNuapada, districts; \n(c)the details of fund al located for this scheme under the Odisha \nState, district-wise; and \n(d)the details of facilities being provided to labourers for \nrehabilitation? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n \n(a) to (d): The Ministry of Labour & Employment has been \nimplementing a Central Sector Scheme viz. Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer for identification and rehabilitation of bonded labourers. \nThe Scheme was last revamped in January, 2022 and henceforth is \nknown as Cen tral Sector Scheme for Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer-2021. The salient features of the scheme are as follows: \n• Under the Scheme, the Government of India provides \nimmediate financial assistance amounting to Rs.30,000/- for \neach case of rehabilitation. \n• Rehabilitation assistance to the tune of Rs.1.00 lakh, \nRs.2.00 lakh and Rs.3.00 lakh is provided to rescued bonded \nlabourers based on their category and level of exploitation \nupon proof of bondage. \nContd..2/- \n\n:: 2 :: \n• The Scheme also provides for financial as sistance of Rs. \n4.50 lakh per district to the States for conducting Survey \nonce in every three years per sensitive district, Rs. 1.50 lakh \nfor evaluatory studies (maximum of five Evaluatory Studies \nper year) and Rs. 10 lakhs per State per annum for \nawareness generation. \n• The Scheme provides for creation of a Bonded Labour \nRehabilitation Fund at District level by each State with a \npermanent corpus of at least Rs. 10  lakh at the disposal of \nthe District Magistrate which would be renewable for \nextending immediate help to the released bonded labourers. \nThe Central Sector Scheme for Rehabilitation of Bonded \nLabourer is a demand driven scheme where funds are re-imbursed to \nconcerned State/UTs Governments on receipt of proposal from them. \nDuring last three financial  years i.e. FY 2020 -21, FY 2021 -22 & FY \n2022-23, the State Government has not sent any  proposal and \nMinistry of Labour & Employment has not released any funds for         \nre-imbursement of financial assistance to the State Government of \nOdisha including districts Kalahandi and Nuapada. \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_106": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*106 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nNATIONAL CAREER SERVICE (NCS) PROJECT \n \n*106.     SHRI P.P. CHAUDHARY: \n             SHRI ANURAG SHARMA: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT  be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the objectives of National Career Service (NCS) Project; \n(b) the number of Employment Exchanges and Model Career \nCentres (MCCs) in the country, State/district-wise; \n(c) the number of Employment Exchanges upgraded to Model Career \nCentres (MCCs) in the country , State-wise and district -wise in \nRajasthan and Uttar Pradesh; \n(d) the status and details of Pali, Jhansi, Deoria, Pratapgarh \nEmployment Exchange and the number of benefic iaries/job \nseekers/ employers registered on NCS por tal in these \nConstituencies;  \n(e) whether the Government has plans for  setting up more \nEmployment Exchange/MCCs and if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) the details of steps taken by the Government to get more \nemployers registered   on the NCS portal? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BHUPENDER YADAV) \n \n(a) to (f): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n*** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (f) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *106 DUE FOR REPLY ON 11.12.202 3 \nREGARDING “NATIONAL CAREER SERVICE  (NCS) PROJECT” BY SHRI \nP.P. CHAUDHARY AND SHRI ANURAG SHARMA:  \n(a) to (f) :   Ministry of Labour  and Employment is implementing the \nNational Career Service (NCS) project to provide a variety of career \nrelated services like job search and matching, career counselling, \nvocational guidance, information on skill development courses etc. \nthrough a digital platform [www.ncs.gov.in].  \n \n          Establishment/Expansion of Employment Exchanges is under the \npurview of the State/UT Governments. As on 30.11.2023 there are 1005 \nEmployment Exchanges in the country. Under the NCS project, the \nGovernment has targeted to establish 400 Model Career Centres \n(MCCs) and all 400 MCCs have been approved including 370 in \nEmployment Exchanges. State -wise details of Employment Exchanges \nand Model Career Centres are provided at Annexure I and Location -\nwise employment exchanges of Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh where \nModel Career Centres have been approved are provided at Annexure-II.  \n \nThe number of beneficiaries/Jobseekers/Employers register ed on \nNCS portal of Pali, Jhansi, Deoria and Pratapgargh districts as on 30th \nNovember 2023 are given below: \n \n \nSl. No. Districts Jobseeker Employer \n1 Pali 1,28,835 7,804 \n2 Jhansi 1,01,710 2,172 \n3 Deoria 70,445 2,072 \n4 Pratapgarh 1,46,898 2,741 \n\n           In order to increase the registration of employers on the NCS \nportal, the Government has taken the following initiatives:  \ni. NCS portal has  been integrated with Udyam portal of Ministry of \nMicro, Small and Medium Enterprises  (MSME) for seamless \nregistration of Udyam registered MSMEs as employers on the NCS \nportal. More than 16.81 lakhs Udyam registered MSMEs are \nregistered as employers on NCS portal. \nii. Ministry of Labour and Employment has signed Memorandum of \nUnderstandings (MoUs)/ partnerships with  various job portals/ \nprivate organizations  like Monster.com, Freshersworld, Quess \nCorp Limited, HireMee, TCS -iON, UNICEF YuWaah etc to enhance \njob opportunities on NCS for the job seekers.   \niii. NCS portal is also interlinked with EPFO and ESIC portals for \nconnecting with the employers.   \niv. Further, to popularize NCS and for connecting with job seekers \nand employers, Information Education and Communication (IEC) \nactivities and outreach activities are regularly organized. \n \n*****  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY (a) TO (f) TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *106 DUE FOR REPLY ON 11.12.2023 \n \nSl. \nNo \nState/UT No. of \nEmployme\nnt \nExchange \nNo. of \nEmployment \nExchanges \nupgraded to \nMCCs  \nNo. of MCCs \napproved at \ninstitutions \nother than \nEmployment \nExchanges \nTotal \nNo. of \nMCCs \n1 Arunachal Pradesh 12 6 - 6 \n2 Assam 52 12 1 13 \n3 Andaman & Nicobar Islands 1 1 - 1 \n4 Andhra Pradesh 17 29 1 30 \n5 Bihar 47 20 - 20 \n6 Chhattisgarh 26 12 - 12 \n7 Delhi 3 1 1 2 \n8 Goa 1 1 - 1 \n9 Gujarat 46 13 - 13 \n10 Haryana 65 3 1 4 \n11 Himachal Pradesh 15 2 - 2 \n12 Jammu & Kashmir 20 18 1 19 \n13 Ladakh 2 2 1 3 \n14 Jharkhand 42 20 - 20 \n15 Karnataka 40 13 1 14 \n16 Kerala 89 13 1 14 \n17 Lakshadweep 1 1 1 2 \n18 Maharashtra 36 26 2 28 \n19 Meghalaya 12 4 - 4 \n20 Madhya Pradesh 49 11 2 13 \n21 Manipur 11 1 1 2 \n22 Mizoram 3 4 - 4 \n23 Nagaland 8 7 - 7 \n24 Odisha 40 30 2 32 \n25 Puducherry 1 4 - 4 \n26 Punjab 47 12 - 12 \n27 Rajasthan 38 16 - 16 \n28 Sikkim - - 6 6 \n29 Telangana 34 12 - 12 \n30 Tripura 6 3 1 4 \n31 Tamil Nadu 35 19 1 20 \n32 Uttar Pradesh 100 35 4 39 \n33 Uttarakhand 24 4 1 5 \n34 West Bengal 77 15 1 16 \n35 Chandigarh  2 - - - \n36 D. &N. Haveli Daman & Diu 3 - - - \nTotal 1005 370 30 400 \nThe State-wise number of Employment Exchanges and Model Career Centres  \n\n \nANNEXURE-II \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY (a) TO (f) TO THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *106 DUE FOR REPLY ON 11.12.2023 \n \nThe number of Employment Exchanges where Model Career Centers \n(MCCs) have been approved in Rajasthan and Uttar Pradesh. \n \na. Rajasthan: \nSl. No. Location \n1 Alwar, Employment Exchange \n2 Banswara District Employment Exchange \n3 Baran District Employment Exchange \n4 Bharatpur, Employment Exchange \n5 Bikaner, Employment Exchange \n6 Dausa, Employment Exchange \n7 Jaipur, Employment Exchange \n8 Jaisalmer District Employment Exchange \n9 Jalore District Employment Exchange \n10 Jhalawar, Employment Exchange \n11 Jhunjhunu, Employment Exchange \n12 Kota, Employment Exchange \n13 Pali, Employment Exchange \n14 Sawai Madhopur, Employment Exchange \n15 Sirohi, Employment Exchange \n16 Sri Ganga Nagar District Employment Exchange \n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n�\n\n�\n�\nb. Uttar Pradesh: \nSl. No. Location \n1 Agra, Regional Employment Exchange \n2 Aligarh Employment Exchange \n3 Bahraich Employment Exchange \n4 Banaras Hindu University \n5 Bareilly Regional Employment Exchange \n6 Basti Regional Employment Exchange \n7 Bijnore District Employment Exchange \n8 Bulandshahar Employment Exchange \n9 Bundelkhand University, Jhansi \n10 Deoria Employment Exchange \n11 Etawah Employment Exchange \n12 Faiazabad, Regional Employment Exchange \n13 Fatehpur Employment Exchange \n14 Firozabad Employment Exchange \n15 Ghaziabad, Employment Exchange \n16 Gonda Regional Employment Exchange \n17 Hardoi District Employment Exchange \n18 Kanpur Dehat Employment Exchange \n19 Kushinagar Employment Exchange \n20 Lakhimpur Khiri District Employment Exchange \n21 Lucknow Employment Exchange \n22 Mahoba, Employment Exhange \n23 Mathura, Employment Exhange \n24 Meerut, Employment Exchange \n25 Mirzapur, Regional Employment Exchange \n26 Moradabad, Employment Exchange \n27 Muzaffarnagar Regional Employment Exchange \n28 Pilibhit Employment Exchange \n29 Pratapgarh Employment Exchange \n30 Raebareli Employment Exchange \n31 Saharanpur Regional Employment Exchange \n32 Sitapur District Employment Exchange \n33 Sonebhadra Employment Exchange \n34 Sultanpur Employment Exchange \n35 University of Allahabad, Allahabad \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_107": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO- †*107 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON- 11/12/2023 \n \nRESERVATION OF TRIBAL TRADITIONS \n  \n  \n†*107. SHRI SANJAY SETH: \nWill the Minister of TRIBAL AFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any steps have been taken by the Government to preserve tribal traditions, culture and \ncustoms; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether due to conversion there has been an adverse impact on tribal traditions, culture and customs; \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof including steps taken by the Government to stop conversion;  \n \n(e) whether any steps have been taken by the Government to stop conversion of tribal community in the \ncountry particularly in Jharkhand as conversion is a big issue in tribal dominated States; \n  \n(f)  whether the Government has any data of tribals converted in Jharkhand during the last five years; \nand  \n  \n(g) if so, the details thereof? \n  \n  \n  \nANSWER \n  \nMINISTER OF TRIBAL AFFAIRS \n(SHRI ARJUN MUNDA) \n  \n(a) to (g):        A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n***** \n  \n  \n  \n\n \n  \nStatement referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Starred Question No. †*107 for answer on \n11.12.2023 \n(a) & (b): Yes, Sir. Ministry of Tribal Affairs has taken various steps to preserve tribal traditions, culture \nand customs through Centrally Sponsored Schemes of “Support to Tribal Research Institute” and Central \nSector Scheme “Tribal Research, Information, Education, Communication and Events” under which \nvarious activities are undertaken to conserve and promote tribal culture, archives, artifacts, customs and \ntraditions of the tribal communities. Under the scheme “Support to Tribal Research Institutes (TRIs)”, \nMinistry has sanctioned various projects to the Tribal Research Institutes of the States/UTs towards \nvarious activities for socio-cultural and economic development of the tribal communities including \npreservation of their cultural heritage and protection of the indigenous faith, practices, unique ideas and \nknowledge and of tribal people in diverse form. There are 28 Tribal Research Institutes in States/UTs \nand National Tribal Research Institute in Delhi. Some of the initiatives worth mentioning are as under: \ni. To acknowledge the heroic and patriotic deeds of tribal people and to exhibit rich tribal cultural \nheritage of the region, Ministry has sanctioned 10 Tribal Freedom Fighters Museums. \nii. The Ethnographic Museum under the States’ TRIs have been sanctioned to preserve and \nshowcase rare artifacts related to life and culture of various tribes.  \niii. Tribal Research Institutes have organized different events like National Tribal Craft Mela, \nNational/State Tribal Dance Festival, Art Competition, Workshop – cum – Exhibition on Tribal \npaintings and state level tribal poet and writers meet. For promoting the unique tribal heritage, \nMinistry has sanctioned fund to organize the tribal fairs & festivals, festival like \"Medaram \nJathara\" undertaken by the Koya Tribe of Telangana, Hornbill Festival of Nagaland, Sarhul \nFestival of Jharkhand, Lokotsav of Goa and Pawl Kut festival of Mizoram, etc. \niv. Ministry has developed searchable digital repository where all research \npapers, books, reports and documents, folks songs, photos/videos are uploaded. The repository \ncurrently has more than 10,000 photographs, videos and publications which are mostly done by \nTribal Research Institutes.    \nv. Under the scheme “Tribal Research, Information, Education, Communication and Events (TRI-\nECE)”, reputed research institutes/organizations/universities have undertaken various research \nstudies/publication of books/documentation including audio visual documentaries to fill the gap \nof research studies on tribal issues and Promotion of rich tribal cultural, traditions and customs \nas well as capacity building of tribal persons/institutions associated with tribal affairs, \ndissemination of information and creation of awareness. Tribal cultural exchange programmes \nwas also sanctioned. Research and documentation of indigenous practices by tribal healers and \nmedicinal plants, tribal languages, agriculture system, dances and paintings, organization of \nliterary festivals, publication of books written by tribal writers/ authors, translation works and \nliterature competitions, were undertaken under the scheme. Military Tattoo and Tribal Dance \nFestival was organized in January 2023 to exhibit the various dance performances by \ntribal    communities showcasing the beauty of India’s diverse tribal culture from across India.  \nvi. Government of India has declared 15th November as Janjatiya Gaurav Divas to honour all the \ntribal freedom fighters and for remembering and acknowledging their contribution to the \nfreedom struggle and cultural heritage, and to re-energize the efforts for the socio-economic \n\ndevelopment of the tribal regions. The Ministry of Tribal Affairs along with others central \nministries, state governments and other institutions are celebrating the glorious history of its \ntribal people, culture and achievements since 2021. \nvii. Development of bilingual Primers for preservation of tribal languages and enhancement of \nlearning achievement level amongst the Scheduled Tribe Students. Many language primers have \nbeen developed by various State Governments. \nviii. Tribal dances are reflection of culture embodying the history, beliefs and tradition of people. \nThese dances are very close to life and nature. Military Tattoo and Tribal Dance Festival was \norganized in January 2023 to exhibit the various dance performances by tribal communities \nshowcasing the beauty of India’s diverse tribal culture from across India. Tribal Dance \npresentation on January 23 & 24, 2023 is at Annexure-I. State-wise list of dances, which are \npreserved and promoted through their participation in various cultural events/other events is at \nAnnexure-II. \n     The Ministry of Culture is the nodal Ministry for promotion of culture \n         including tribal culture. \n   \n            Further, there are adequate constitutional and statutory safeguards for protection, preservation, \nconservation and promotion of tribal culture, traditions and customs. The Fifth Schedule of the \nConstitution provides for the establishment of Tribal Advisory Councils in States with Scheduled Areas. \nFurther, it provides for Special Powers of the Governor in such States. The Panchayat (Extension to \nScheduled Areas) Act, 1996 similarly provides for extensive authority of the Gram Sabhas/Gram \nPanchayats to safeguard and preserve the traditions and customs and their cultural identity. The Sixth \nSchedule, applicable in the States of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram, empowers the District \nand Regional Councils in matters of social customs. \n  \n(c) to (e): ‘Public Order’ and ‘Police’ are State subjects as per the Seventh Schedule to the Constitution \nof India. Hence, prevention, detection, registration, investigation and prosecution of offences related to \nreligious conversions are primarily the concerns of the State Government/ Union Territory (UT) \nAdministration. Action is taken as per existing laws by the law enforcing agencies whenever instances \nof violation come to notice.  \n(f): Figures about religious conversion are not being maintained Centrally  \n(g): Does not arise in view of (f).     \n     \n  \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \n \nStatement referred to in reply to part (a) and (b) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. †* 107 \nfor answer on 11.12.2023 \nTribal Dance presentation on January 23 & 24, 2023 \n  \nS.No. Name of State Name of Art Form \n1 Mizoram Cherow \n2 Chhattisgarh Gaur Maria \n3 Kerala Irula \n4 UT of Ladakh Balti \n5 Tamilnadu Palaiyar \n6 Jharkhand Paika \n7 Telangana/ Andhra Pradesh Lambadi \n8 Andhra Pradesh Budigali \n9 Jharkhand Karma \n10 UT of Jammu and Kashmir Mangho \n11 Madhya Pradesh Baiga Pardhoni \n12 Gujarat Siddhi Dhamal \n13 Arunachal Pradesh Rikhampada \n14 Telangana Gussadi \n15 Himachal Pradesh Gaddi Nati \n16 Meghalaya Ka Shad Masti \n17 Assam Bagurumba \n18 Maharashtra Songi Mukhote \n19 West Bengal Purulia Chhau \n20 Gujarat Rathwa \n  \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to part (a) and (b) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. †*107 \nfor answer on 11.12.2023 \nAnnexure-II \nIllustrative State wise list of dances \nSl. No. Name of Tribal Dance State \n1. Popir Arunachal Pradesh \n2. Rekham Pada Arunachal Pradesh \n3. Gumrag Assam \n4. Mishawa Assam \n5. Bagurumba Assam \n6. Talwar Nrutya Gujarat \n7. Kahadiya Gujarat \n8. Rathwa Gujarat \n9. Wangala Meghalaya \n10. Kashad Mastieh Meghalaya \n11. Birli Odisha \n12. Dhemsa Odisha \n13. Biha Odisha \n14. Wija Pandum Odisha \n15. Benta Odisha \n16. Rinjodi Odisha \n17. Budigali Odisha \n18. Hojagiri Tripura \n19. Mamita Tripura \n20. Karam Thapadi Jharkhand \n  \n***** \n  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_108": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO-108 \nANSWERED ON-04/12/2023 \n \nMUSLIM COMMUNITY LAGGING BEHIND IN HEIs \n \n108. SHRI NATARAJAN P.R.: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the All India Survey on Education conducted by the Ministry reveals that the \nMuslim community is lagging behind all communities in the Higher Education painting a \ndismal picture of the community; \n \n(b) if so, the details of All India Survey on Education 2020 -21 State -wise/year \nwise/community- wise; and \n \n(c) the proposal of the Government to increase the admission from the communities which \nare lagging behind? \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n \n(DR. SUBHAS SARKAR) \n \n(a) &(b) As per the All India Survey on Higher Education (AISHE), enrolment of students of \nMuslim community in Higher Educational Institutions (HEIs) has increased to 21 .08 Lakh in \n2021-22(Provisional) from 19.22 Lakh in 2020-21, and 15.34 Lakh in 2014 -15. So increase \nin the enrolment of students of Muslim community is around 37.5%, whereas the overall \nStudent enrolment in Higher Education has increased by 26.5% since 2014 -15.  Hence, the \ngrowth in enrolment of Muslim Students is higher the overall student growth.  The details of \nEnrolment of students of Muslim Minority and total Students, State -wise, as per AISHE,  is \navailable at https://www.education.gov.in/parl_ques . \n \n(c) The various initiatives of the Government in encouraging students from the Minority \ncommunity as well as students from SC/ST/OBC communities has resulted in higher growth \nin enrolment of these communities over the years. Some of these initiatives are as under :-  \nI Ministry of Minority Affairs implements the Post Metric and Merit -cum-Means based \nScholarship Schemes for educational empowerment of students belonging to 6 notified \nMinority communities, namely, Buddhist, Christian, Jain, Muslim, Sikh and Zoroastrian  \n(Parsi).  These are implemented with the objective to uplift the socio -economic conditions of \nthe minority communities, increase their rate of attainment in higher education and enhance \ntheir employability and to enable the poor and meritorious students pursue professional and \ntechnical courses.   \n \n \n\nII.  The Department of Higher Education is implementing the “Pradhan Mantri Uchchatar \nShiksha Protsahan [PM-USP] Yojana”, with three component schemes namely :- \n \n• Central Sector Scheme of Scholarship for College and University Students;  \n• Special Scholarship Scheme for Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh aimed specifically at \nyouth of Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh to take advantage of the educational \ninstitutions outside these UTs  and interact with their counterparts from rest of \ncountry]; \n• Central Sector Interest Subsidy Scheme.  \nAll categories of students in the country including students from Muslim \nCommunity are eligible to get financial assistance as per the schematic norms.  \n[The details of these schemes are available in the Ministry’s website: \nhttps://www.education.gov.in/en/pm-usp-scholarships-education-loan].  \nIII.  Besides, various fellowships and scholarships are being implemented  University Grants \nCommission, All India Council for Technical Education, Ministry of Social Justice & \nEmpowerment and Ministry of Tribal Affairs.  \nIV. Examinations like Joint Entrance Examination (JEE), National Eligibility cum Entrance \nTest (NEET) etc. are conducted in 13 Indian languages, to facilitate the students of \nSC/ST/OBC/rural/vernacular background. \nV. Education and Skill Textbooks in Indian languages are being brought ou t. All India \nCouncil of Higher Education (AICTE) has released entire course books for 1 st Year of \nEngineering and Polytechnic subjects in Indian Languages. These would facilitate students, \nparticularly from vernacular background and marginalised communities in pursuing Higher \nStudies.  \nVI. The University Grants Commission is implementing the following Schemes for weaker \nsections, including minorities : \ni. Remedial Coaching for SC/ST/OBC (non -creamy layer), and  Minority Community \nStudents \nii. Coaching  Classes  for  Entry  into Services for SC/ST/OBC (Non Creamy Layer) and \nMinority Community Students \niii. Coaching For National Eligibility Test or State Eligibility Test for SC/ST/OBC (Non-\nCreams Layer), and Minority Community Students.  \niv. Certain relaxations in eligibility conditions in UGC-Junior Research Fellowship    for \nSC/ST students are provided. \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_109": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*109 \nANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nPROMOTION OF WILDLIFE AND FOREST TOURISM \n \n*109.  DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE: \n  SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI: \n  \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether any scheme has been formulated for promotion of wildlife \nand forest tourism in the country;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the scope of the same in \nvarious States/UTs including Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu;  \n(c) whether there is a big potent ial for wildlife and forest tourism in \nthe country;  \n(d) if so, the steps taken to tap the potential and for promotion of the \nwildlife and forest tourism in the notified Tiger Reserves, National \nParks and Wildlife Sanctuaries within the country including \nMaharashtra State;  \n(e) whether any scheme has been put in place to involve local \ncommunities as stakeholders in the development of sustainable \nwildlife and forest tourism; and  \n(f) if so, the details thereof and the funds provided by the \nGovernment for pro moting sustainable wildlife and forest tourism \nduring the last five years, State/UT -wise including Maharashtra \nand Tamil Nadu State?  \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM         (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (f): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n******* \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a)  TO (f) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION No.*109 ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 REGARDING PROMOTION \nOF WILDLIFE AND FOREST TOURISM BY DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE \nAND SHRI C.N. ANNADURAI. \n \n(a) to (f): The Ministry of Tourism under  its scheme of Swadesh \nDarshan identified Wildlife Circuit and Eco Circuit as among 15 thematic \ncircuits for development.  The above circuits cover National Parks, \nWildlife Sanctuaries and Reserves in the country.  The details of projects \nsanctioned under the Wildlife and Eco Circuits of Swadesh Darshan \nscheme in various States/UTs is at Annexure.  No project has been \nsanctioned in the State s of Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu under the \nabove two circuits of the scheme. \n \nMinistry of Tourism has now revamped its Swadesh Darshan scheme as \nSwadesh Darshan 2.0 (SD 2.0) with the objective to develop sustainable \nand responsible destinations following a tourist and destination centric \napproach.  Sindhudurg in Maharashtra and  Mamallapuram and The \nNilgirs in Tamil Nadu have been identified for development under the \nSwadesh Darshan 2.0 scheme.  \n \nApart from the above, Ministry of Environment, Forest & Climate Change \n(MoEFCC) has conveyed the following:- \n \n(i) The Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972, empowers the States/UTs, \nincluding Maharashtra and Tamil Nadu, for declaration of an \narea as Sanctuary and National Park, if it considers such area is \nof adequate ecological, faunal, floral, geo -morphological, \nnatural or zoological significance. The management of such \ndesignated areas  (National Parks, Sanctuaries, Tiger Reserve) \nis within the ambit of the respective States/UTs. Such \n\nmanagement interventions may also include Eco -Tourism \nactivities in these areas. The Ministry has issued guidelines for \npreparation of management plans for these areas in accordance \nwith the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 on 30.11.2023. \n(ii) The Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change has \nissued ‘Guidelines for Sustainable Ecotourism in Forest and \nWildlife Areas-2021’ on 29.10.2021. It promotes engagement of \nlocal communities in nature tourism in a manner that enriches \nlocal economy and encourages sustainable use of indigenous \nmaterials through financially viable values chains thereby \nhelping such local communities become “Atma Nirbhar”.  The \nGuidelines also includes potential sites of Eco Tourism in the \ncountry, including Sanjay Gandhi (Borivilli), Lonar and Thane \nCreek Flamingo in Maharashtra and Gulf of Mannar Marine, \nVedanthangal and Point Calimere in Tamil Nadu. \n(iii) Further, MoEFCC has also circulated guidelines for preparation \nof management plans for protected areas which also includes \ntourism activities.  \n(iv) The National Tiger Conservation Authority has also notified the \n‘Normative Standards for tourism activities and Project Tiger \nguidelines, 2012’. \n \nIn addition to above, Ministry of Tourism has formulated National \nStrategy for Sustainable Tourism.  Following strategic pillars have been \nidentified for development of sustainable tourism: \n \n(i) Promoting Environmental Sustainability \n(ii) Protecting Biodiversity  \n(iii) Promoting Economic Sustainability  \n(iv) Promoting Socio-Cultural Sustainability  \n\n(v) Scheme for Certification of Sustainable Tourism \n(vi) IEC and Capacity Building  \n(vii) Governance  \n \nAlso, as part of its on -going activities, the Ministry of Tourism  regularly \npromotes India as a holistic tourism destination including wildlife and \nforest tourism  through various mediums including social media and \nthrough its website www.incredibleindia.org. \n \n******* \n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a)  TO (f) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION No.*109 ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 REGARDING PROMOTION OF \nWILDLIFE AND FOREST TOURISM BY DR. AMOL RAMSING KOLHE AND SHRI \nC.N. ANNADURAI \n \nDetails of projects sanction ed under the Wildlife and Eco Circuits of \nSwadesh Darshan Scheme in the country \n \nWILDLIFE CIRCUIT            (Rupees in crore) \nS. \nNo. \nName of \nState \nYear of \nsanction \nProject Name Amount \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \n1. Madhya \nPradesh \n2015-16 Development of \nCircuit at Panna - \nMukundpur- Sanjay- \nDubri-Bandhavgarh- \nKanha- Mukki- Pench. \n92.10 86.31 \n \n2. Assam 2015-16 Development of \nManas– Pobitora– \nNameri– Kaziranga– \nDibru– Saikhowa. \n94.68 89.94 \nTotal 186.78 176.25 \n \n \nECO CIRCUIT             (Rupees in crore) \nS. No. Name of \nState \nYear of \nsanction \nProject Name Amount \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \n1. Uttarakhand 2015-16 Development of \nCircuit at Tehri -\nChamba- Sarain \naround Tehri Lake. \n69.17 69.17 \n2. Telangana 2015-16 Development of \nCircuit in \nMahaboobnagar \ndistricts ( Somasila, \nSingotam, \nKadalaivanam, \nAkkamahadevi, \nEgalanpanta, \nFarahabad, Uma \nMaheshwaram, \n91.62 91.25 \n \n\nMallelatheertham) \n3. Kerala 2015-16 Development of \nPathanamthitta- Gavi- \nVagamon- Thekkady. \n64.08 64.08 \n4. Mizoram 2016-17 Development of Eco -\nAdventure Circuit at \nAizawl -Rawpuichhip - \nKhawhphawp - \nLengpui – Durtlang -\nChatlang- \nSakawrhmuituaitlang \n- Muthee – \nBeratlawng -Tuirial \nAirfield – Hmuifang \n66.37 53.09 \n5. Madhya \nPradesh \n2017-18 Development of \nGandhisagar Dam - \nMandleshwar Dam - \nOmkareshwar Dam - \nIndira Sagar Dam - \nTawa Dam - Bargi \nDam- BhedaGhat- \nBansagar Dam - Ken \nRiver \n93.76 \n \n89.07 \n6. Jharkhand 2018-19 Development of \nDalma- Chandil- \nGetalsud- \nBetlaNational park - \nMirchaiya- Netarhat \n30.44 28.83 \nTotal 415.44 395.49 \n \n \n********** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_11": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.11 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nPERMISSION TO GRADE-1 DIABETIC STUDENTS IN CLASSROOM & \nEXAMINATION \n \n*11. SHRI SYED IMTIAZ JALEEL: \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware of the decision taken by the Maharashtra Education \nDepartment recently to permit students suffering from Type -1 Diabetes in grades I to XII \nto consume snacks and take medication in the classroom and also during examinations, \nconsidering them as part of the special needs (disabled) category, if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b) whether the Government has information on students being allowed to use devices such \nas Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM), Flash Glucose Monitoring (FGM) or insulin \npumps during exams as communicated by the Education Department; \n(c) if so, the details thereof;  \n(d) whether the Government endorses the provision that if a smartphone is being used for \nglucose monitoring, it should be kept with the teacher or invigilator only; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF EDUCATION \n(SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO ( E) OF THE LOK \nSABHA ADMITTED STARRED QUESTION NO. 11 FOR REPLY ON 04.12.2023 \nASKED BY HON’BLE MP SHRI SYED IMTIAZ JALEEL REGARDING ‘PERMISSION \nTO GRADE-1 DIABETIC STUDENTS IN CLASSROOM & EXAMINATION’.  \n \n(a) to (e) Education is in the concurrent list of the Constitution and majority of the schools are \nunder the domain of respective State and UT Government s. State and UT Governments are the \nappropriate Governments under the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education (RTE) \nAct, 2009.  \n The Government of Maharashtra vide Government Resolution dated 26.10.2023 \npermitted students suffering from Type-1 Diabetes in grades I to XII to consume snacks and take \nmedication in the  classroom and also during examinations.  The students suffering from Type -1 \nDiabetes can avail these facilities on demand. \n Para 22.24 of Resolution dated 26.10.2023 of Government of Maharashtra mentions that, \nif smartphone is being used for glucose monitoring then it must be kept with the teacher or \ninvigilator.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_110": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*110 \nANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 \n \nCOMPARATIVE ANALYSIS OF EMPLOYMENT IN TOURISM SECTOR \n \n*110.SHRI JAGDAMBIKA PAL:   \n                                            \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of comparative analysis of the current employment \nscenario in the tourism sector in India over the last three years, \nincluding the total number of jobs directly generated and the \nmanner in which this has evolved over the abovementioned period;  \n(b) the comparative overview of its contribution to the Gross Domestic \nProduct (GDP) of India over the past three years; and \n(c) the trends or changes noticed during the last three years? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM                 (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n \n****** \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO.* 110 ANSWERED ON 11.12.2023 REGARDING \nCOMPARATIVE ANALYSIS OF EMPLOYMENT IN TOURISM SECTOR BY \nSHRI. JAGDAMBIKA PAL \n \n(a) to  (c): The employment scenario in the tourism sector and the \ncontribution of tourism to the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) in India, \nare as follows: \n \nIndicator 2019-20 2020-21 2021-22 \nTotal Tourism GDP (Rs. crore) 1041747.04 297432.99 415689.03 \nTotal Tourism share in GDP \n(%) \n5.18 1.50* 1.77 \nTotal Tourism Jobs (million) 69.44 68.07 70.04 \nShare in employment 13.50 12.91* 12.66 \n \nSource: National Council of Applied Economic Research (NCAER)  \ncomputations using National Acc ount Statistics 2023, Third Tourism \nSatellite Account - 2015-16 and respective rounds of Periodic Labour \nForce Survey data. \n*The decline in the share of tourism GDP and tourism employment is due \nto the COVID-19 pandemic. \n \nThe share of tourism in the GDP de clined in the year 2020 -21 to 1.50 % \nfrom 5.18% in the year 2019 -20 due to the Covid pandemic. However, in \nthe post-pandemic year 2021 -22, the share of tourism in the total GDP \nincreased to 1.77 %. The share of Tourism emplo yment in the total \nemployment of the economy declined in the year 2020-21 to 12.91% from \n13.50% in the year 2019 -20. In the post -pandemic year 2021 -22, the \nshare of Tourism employment was 12.66%. \n******** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_111": "Government of India \nMinistry of Finance \nDepartment of Financial services \n \nLOK SABHA  \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 111 \nANSWERED ON DECEMBER 4th, 2023/ AGRAHAYANA 13, 1945 (SAKA) \nPRADHAN MANTRI MUDRA YOJANA (PMMY) \n \n111.  SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL: \n         SHRI ARUN KUMAR SAGAR: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the nationalized banks have organized the programmes related to centrally operated \nschemes like Pradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana, Stand Up India Yojana, Pradhan Mantri Jeevan \nJyoti Bima  Yojana (PMJJBY), Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima Yojana (PMSBY), Atal \nPension Yojana etc., in Meerut and Shahjahanpur Constituencies of Uttar Pradesh; \n \n(b) if so, the place -wise/date-wise details of the programmes organized in Meerut Constituency \nduring the last three years; \n \n(c) whether the nationalized banks have invited the local public representatives in each programme \norganized in Meerut Constituency during the above period; \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof; \n \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(f) the steps taken by the  Government to ensure participation of local representatives in the \nprogrammes related to the operation of central schemes by the nationalized banks? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(Dr. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n \n(a)  Banks have organised various programmes in Meerut and Shahjahanpur constituencies of Uttar \nPradesh in collaboration with the local administration and other stake holders for Schemes like \nPradhan Mantri Mudra Yojana (PMMY), Stand Up India Yojana (SUPI), Pra dhan Mantri Jeevan \nJyoti Bima Yojana (PMJJBY), Pradhan Mantri Suraksha Bima Yojana (PMSBY) and Atal Pension \nYojana (APY) etc. \n \n(b) to (f) State Level Bankers’ Committee (SLBC), Uttar Pradesh (UP) has informed that \ninvitations are sent to the various categories of public representatives while organising \nprogrammes under these Schemes.  \nDetails of the programmes organised wherein the public representatives participated in the Meerut \nConstituency during the last three years is placed at Annexure I.  \nFurther, Department of Financial Services has also written to SLBC, UP to ensure the presence of \nPublic Representatives viz. MPs/MLAs etc . in the meetings of the District Level Review \nCommittees (DLRCs) and also in all the Credit Outreach programmes under   various Government \nSchemes.   \n***** \n  \n\n6482 GI/2023 (1) \n \n�������������������������������������33004/99� REGD. No. D. L.-33004/99 \n \n \n \nxxxGIDHxxx \nxxxGIDExxx \n�����������������\nEXTRAORDINARY \n��������II�������� ����3������������� ����(ii)�� \nPART II—Section 3—Sub-section (ii) \n�������������� ���� ������������ \nPUBLISHED BY AUTHORITY \n��������������, ���� ���� ������������ �������������� �������������\n���������������� \n���� ����������, 12 ������ ��������, 2023 \n���������� 4458(��).—���������������������������������������������������������� �� ������������������������������������������������������������������������ �� ������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ������������������������������������������������������������;  \n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ������������, ������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� ����������������������������������, ����������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������, ��������������������������������������'��������' - \n'��������������������������������������������' ���������������� ��������;  \n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������“��������” \n������������������������������������������������������������������������, �������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ��������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ ����������������������������������������������������������������������; \n���������������� �� ��������������������, ������������������������������������, ���������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ��������\n���������������� ������, ������������������������������, ���������� �� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������, \n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ������������������������������������������������ �� ��������������������������������������\n��������, ���������� ���������������������������������������������������������������������� �� ������������������������������������������ �� ����; \n����.   4287]� ����������������,������������ ������������,�������� ����������12, 2023���������� ������20, 1945�  \nNo. 4287] NEW DELHI, THURSDAY, OCTOBER 12, 2023/ASVINA 20, 1945  \n����������������������������������������������������������������\nCG-DL-E-13102023-249377\n\n2  THE GAZETTE OF INDIA : EXTRAORDINARY    [PART II—SEC. 3(ii)] \nऔर  ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम कार्ाान्वर्न जनर्म, 2023 का  प्रारूप भारत  के  रािपत्र, असाधारण , भाग  II, खंड  3, \nउप-खंड  (ii) में अजधसूचना  संख्र्ा का .आ. 2779 (अ), तारीख  26 िून, 2023 द्वारा प्रकाजित दकर्ा  गर्ा  था  जिसमें सभी \nव्यजक्तर्ों से आपजिर्ााँ और  सुझाव  आमंजत्रत दकए  गए थे; \nऔर  क ें द्रीर् सरकार  द्वारा प्राप्त सभी आपजिर्ों और  सुझावों  पर  सम् र्क  रूप  से जवचार  दकर्ा  गर्ा  िै; \nअतः अब, क ें द्रीर् सरकार  पर्ाावरण  (संरक्षण) अजधजनर्म, 1986 (1986 का  29), धारा  3, धारा  6 और  धारा  25 \nद्वारा प्रिि िजक्तर्ों का  प्रर्ोग करते  हुए जनम्नजलजखत जनर्म बनाती  िै, अथाात्: - \n1. संजक्षप्त नाम  और  प्रारंभ — (1) इन जनर्मों का  संजक्षप् त नाम  ग्रीन क्र े जडट जनर्म, 2023 िै।  \n (2) र्े रािपत्र में इनक े प्रकािन की  तारीख  को  प्रवृि िोंगे। \n2. ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम के  उद्देश्र्. - (1) ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम बािार -आधाररत  तंत्र के  माध्र्म से पर्ाावरण  िेतु सकारात्मक  \nकार्ों  को  प्रोत्साजित करे गा  और  ग्रीन क्र े जडट प्रिान करेगा , जिससे व्यापार कर  पाएंगे  और  र्े घरेलू  बािार  मंच पर  व्यापार \nिेतु उपलब्ध कराए  िाएंगे।  \n(2) ग्रीन क्र े जडट जनर्म 4 के  उप-जनर्म (2) में जनर्िाि दकसी  भी पर्ाावरणीर्  कार्ा  के  जलए दकसी  व्यजक्त द्वारा दकए  \nगए उपार्  से प्राप्त िोगा।  \n(3) ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम उद्योगों, क ं पजनर्ों और  अन्र् संस्ट्थाओं को  वतामान  में प्रवृि दकसी  भी जवजध के  अंतगात \nजवद्यमान िाजर्त्वों र्ा  अन् र् िाजर्त्वों को  पूरा  करने  के  साथ -साथ  जनर्म 4 में जनर्िाि स्ट्वैजछिक पर्ाावरणीर्  उपार्  \nकरने  िेतु अन् र् व्यजक्तर्ों, और  इकाइर्ों  को  सुजवधा  प्रिान करने  के  जलए ग्रीन क्र े जडट प्रिान कर  र्ा  खरीिकर  \nप्रोत्साजित करेगा ;  \nपरन् तु दक  उस समर् प्रवृि जवजध की  अनुपालना  में पूरे  दकए  गए िाजर्त्व से सृजित र्ा  प्राप्त ग्रीन क्र े जडट व्यापार र्ोग्र् \nनिीं िोगा।  \n3. पररभाषाएाँ - (1) इन जनर्मों में, िब तक  दक  संिभा में अन्र्था अपेजक्षत न िो, - \n(क ) \"प्रिासक\" से जनर्म 7 में जनर्िाि प्रिासक अजभप्रेत िै;  \n(ख) \"अजभजित अजभकरण \" से जनर्म 13 के  अधीन ऐसी अजभजित कोई  इकाई  अजभप्रेत िै; \n(ग) \"ग्रीन क्र े जडट\" से दकसी  जवजिि कार्ा , जिसका  पर्ाावरण  पर  सकारात्मक  प्रभाव पड़ता िो, िेतु प्रिान दकए  गए \nप्रोत्सािन की  एकल  इकाई  अजभप्रेत िै; \n(घ) \"रजिस्ट्री\" से तात्पर्ा जनर्म 10 के  अधीन स्ट्थाजपत ग्रीन क्र े जडट रजिस्ट्री अजभप्रेत िै। \n(2) उन िब् िों और  पिों के  िो इन जनर्मों में प्रर्ुक् त िैं और  पररभाजषत  निीं िैं कक ं तु पर्ाावरण  (संरक्षण) अजधजनर्म, \n1986 में पररभाजषत  िैं, विी अथा िोंगे िो उस अजधजनर्म में िै।   \n4. ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम - (1) कोई  व् र्जक्त र्ा  इकाई  पर्ाावरण  की  सुरक्षा, परररक्षण र्ा  संरक्षण के  प्रर्ोिनाथा  \nउप-जनर्म (2) में जनर्िाि कोई  भी उपार्  कर  सक ेगा।  \n(2)  उप-जनर्म (1) में जनर्िाि उपार्ों  में जनम्नजलजखत कार्ा  सजम्मजलत िैं, अथाात्:- \n(i) वृक्षारोपण – सम्पूणा िेि में िररत आवरण  बढाने  के  जलए कार्ों  को  प्रोत्सािन िेतु; \n(ii) िल प्रबंधन - िल संरक्षण, िल संचर्न और  िल के  क ुिल  उपर्ोग र्ा  िल बचत को  प्रोत्सािन िेतु, जिसमें \nअपजिि िल का  उपचार  और पुन: उपर्ोग सजम्मजलत िै; \n(iii) सतत क ृ जष - उत्पािकता, मृिा  स्ट्वास्ट््र् और उत्पादित खाद्य के  पोषण मूल्र् में सुधार  लाने  िेतु प्राक ृ जतक एवं \nपुनर्ोिी क ृ जष प्रथाओं और भूजम पुनरूद्धार  को  प्रोत्सािन िेतु; \n(iv) अपजिि प्रबंधन - संग्रिण, पृथक्करण और पर्ाावरण  की  िृजि से सुिृढ प्रबंधन सजित अपजिि प्रबंधन िेतु \nचक्रीर्ता, सततता  और पररष्क ृ त प्रथाओं को  प्रोत्सािन िेतु; \n(v) वार्ु  प्रिूषण में कमी  लाना  - वार्ु  प्रिूषण में कमी  लाने  वाले  उपार्ों और अन्र् प्रिूषण उपिमन कार्ाकलापों  के  \nप्रोत्सािन िेतु; \n\n[भाग  II—खण् ड 3(ii)] भारत का रािपत्र : असाधारण   3 \n(vi) मैंग्रोव संरक्षण और पुनरूद्धार  - मैंग्रोव के  संरक्षण और पुनरूद्धार  के  उपार्ों को  प्रोत्सािन िेतु; \n(vi) इकोमाक ा  लेबल जवकजसत  करना  - जवजनमााताओं  को  अपने माल  और सेवाओं  िेतु इकोमाक ा  लेबल प्राप्त करने  के  \nजलए प्रोत्साजित करना ; \n(vii) सतत भवन और बुजनर्ािी ढांचा  - पर्ाावरण  अनुक ूल प्रौद्योजगदकर्ों और सामजग्रर्ों का  उपर्ोग करक े  सतत \nभवनों एवं अन्र् आधारभूत  ढांचे  के  जनमााण  को  प्रोत्साजित करना।  \n(3) ग्रीन क्र े जडट प्राप्त करने  के  इछिुक  व्यजक्त र्ा  संस्ट्था को  उप-जनर्म (2) में जनर्िाि दकसी  भी कार्ा  िेतु ग्रीन क्र े जडट लेने के  \nजलए उनक े द्वारा दकए  गए कार्ा  को  प्रिासक  के  पास  रजिस्ट् टर करना  िोगा।  \n(4) उप-जनर्म (3) के  अधीन रजिस्ट् रीकरण  िेतु आवेिन क ें द्रीर् सरकार  द्वारा इस प्रर्ोिनाथा स्ट्थाजपत वेबसाइट  के  माध्र्म \nसे इलेक्रॉजनक  रूप  से प्रिासक को  दकर्ा  िाएगा।  \n(5) उप-जनर्म (4) के  अधीन आवेिन प्राप्त िोने पर, प्रिासक कार्ा  को  दक सी अजभजित अजभकरण  द्वारा सत्र्ाजपत \nकराएगा।  \n(6) अजभजित अजभकरण , इस संबंध में बनाए  गए दििाजनिेिों  के  अनुसार , सत्र्ापन एवं पूिताि , य़थावश्र्क, के  पश्चात, \nआवेिक  द्वारा दकए  गए कार्ों  की  पुजि करते  हुए प्रिासक को  ररपोटा प्रस्ट्तुत करेगा।  \n(7) आवेिक  द्वारा दकए  गए कार्ों  की  पुजि करते  हुए उप-जनर्म (6) के  अधीन ररपोटा प्राप्त िोने पर, प्रिासक आवेिक  को  \nग्रीन क्र े जडट का  प्रमाण पत्र प्रिान करेगा।  \n5. ग्रीन क्र े जडट उत्पन्न करने  की  पद्धजत - (1) दकसी  भी कार्ा  के  संिभा में ग्रीन क्र े जडट की  गणना  करने  की  पद्धजत इस प्रकार \nिोगी, िो प्रिासक की  जसफाररिों  पर  क ें द्रीर् सरकार  द्वारा अजधसूजचत की  िाए  । \n(2) दकए  गए दकसी  भी कार्ा  के  संिभा में ग्रीन क्र े जडट की  गणना  वांजित  पर्ाावरणीर्  पररणाम  प्राप्त करने  िेतु आवश्र्क \nसंसाधन , समान  स्ट्क े ल, क्षेत्र, आकार  एवं अन्र् प्रासंजगक मापिंडों  की  समकक्षता पर  आधाररत  िोगी। \n(3) जनर्म (4) के  अधीन जनर्िाि कार्ों  िेतु ग्रीन क्र े जडट उत्पन्न करने  और  िारी  करने  की  पद्धजतर्ााँ क ें द्रीर् सरकार  द्वारा \nअजधसूजचत की  िाएंगी।  \n6. ग्रीन क्र े जडट उत्पन्न करने  की  प्रदक्रर्ा - (1) ग्रीन क्र े जडट की  गणना  के  प्रर्ोिनाथा दकए  गए कार्ों  के  मूल्र्ांकन और  सत्र्ापन \nकी  पद्धजत इस प्रकार िोगी, िो प्रिासक द्वारा जनधााररत  की  िाए।  \n(2) कार्ों  के  रजिस्ट् रीकरण  का  तरीका  और  इस प्रकार के  रजिस्ट् रीकरण  िेतु अपेजक्षत जववरण प्रिासक द्वारा जनधााररत  \nदकर्ा  िाएगा।  \n(3) प्रिासक कार्ों  के  रजिस्ट् रीकरण , दकए  गए कार्ों  के  मूल्र्ांकन एवं सत्र्ापन और  इस प्रकार सत्र्ाजपत कार्ों  के  के  \nसंबंध में इलेक्रॉजनक  रूप  से ग्रीन क्र े जडट प्रिान करने  िेतु वेबसाइट  जवकजसत  करेगा।  \n7. प्रिासक - (1) भारतीर्  वाजनकी  अनुसंधान  एवं जिक्षा पररषि, सोसार्टी  रजिस्ट् रीकरण  अजधजनर्म, 1860 का  सं. 21 के  \nअधीन रजिस्ट् रीकरण  संख्र्ा 596/1990-91 के  अधीन रजिस्ट् रीकरण  सोसार्टी  तारीख  12 माचा , 1991 द्वारा स्ट्थाजपत और  \nतत्कालीन पर्ाावरण  और  वन मंत्रालर्, भारत  सरकार  द्वारा फा . सं.1-8/89-आरटी , तारीख  22 िून, 1990 के  माध्र्म से \nर्थाघोजषत  एक  स्ट्वार्ि जनकार् , इन जनर्मों के  अधीन प्रिासक िोगा।   \n(2)  प्रिासक इन जनर्मों के  अधीन इसक े प्रबंधन और  संचालन  सजित ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम के  प्रभावी कार्ाान्वर्न के  \nजलए उिरिार्ी  िोगा।  \n(3) प्रिासक के  उिरिाजर्त्वों में जनम्नजलजखत सजम्मजलत िोंगे, अथाात्: - \n(क ) इन जनर्मों के  अधीन ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ा क्रम के  कार्ाान्वर्न के  जलए दििाजनिेि , प्रदक्रर्ाएं और  कार्ा  प्रणाली \nजवकजसत  करना ; \n(ख) पद्धजतर्ां, रजिस्ट् रीकरण  प्रदक्रर्ा, दििाजनिेि  और  संबंजधत माप , ररपोर्टिंग और  सत्र्ापन िेतु तंत्र जवकजसत  \nकरना ;   \n(ग) ग्रीन क्र े जडट (जडजिटल  ग्रीन क्र े जडट िारी  करने  सजित) और  प्रत्र्ेक जचजननत कार्ा  से उत्पन्न ग्रीन क्र े जडट का  \nसमतुल्र् िारी  करने  िेतु कार्ाप्रणाली और  प्रदक्रर्ाएं स्ट्थाजपत करना ;         \n\n4  THE GAZETTE OF INDIA : EXTRAORDINARY    [PART II—SEC. 3(ii)] \n(घ) ग्रीन क्र े जडट रजिस्ट्री और  व्यापार मंच की  स्ट्थापना और  संचालन  िेतु; ग्रीन क्रेजडट  िारी  करने  िेतु दकसी  कार्ा  \nके  रजिस्ट् रीकरण  के  जलए स्ट्व-प्रमाणन र्ा  तृतीर् पक्ष प्रमाणन िेतु; अजभजित अजभकरण  द्वारा इसक े जनरीक्षण और  \nसत्र्ापन िेतु और  लेखा -परीक्षकों का  पैनल बनाने  एवं ऐसे लेखा -परीक्षकों द्वारा लेखा  परीक्षा करने  िेतु \nदििाजनिेि  जवकजसत  करना ; \n(ङ) अनुमोदित दििाजनिेिों  के  अनुसार  ग्रीन क्र े जडट रजिस्ट्री व्यापार मंच सेवा  प्रिाता की  स्ट्थापना र्ा  नाजमत  \nकरना ; \n(च) ग्रीन क्र े जडट प्रोग्राम पोटाल, ज्ञान एवं डेटा  मंच और  रजिस्ट् रीकरण  संस्ट्थाओं से िुल्क के  जलए दििाजनिेि  \nजवकजसत  करना ; \n(ि) नाजमत  अजभकरण , रजिस्ट्री, व्यापार मंच और  ज्ञान एवं डेटा  मंच द्वारा वार्षाक  जववरजणर्ां  एवं प्रगजत \nप्रजतवेिन ििा करने  के  जलए दििाजनिेि  जवकजसत  करना ; \n(ि) ग्रीन क्र े जडट के  व्यापार िेतु बािार  जस्ट्थरता तंत्र के  जलए दििाजनिेि  जवकजसत  करना।  \n(झ) ग्रीन क्र े जडट प्रमाणपत्रों के  व्यापार से संबंजधत मामलों  को  जवजनर्जमत करना  और  जवक्र े ताओं और  क्र े ताओं \nिोनों के  जितों की  रक्षा करना  और  धोखाधड़ी र्ा  अजवश्वास को  रोकने  के  जलए जनवारक  एवं सुधारात्मक  कारावाई  \nकरना।  \n(4) प्रिासक उपरोक्त उपजनर्म (3) के  अधीन दििाजनिेिों  एवं पद्धजतर्ों िेतु क ें द्रीर् सरकार  का  अनुमोिन प्राप्त \nकरेगा।  \n8. संचालन  सजमजत - (1) क ें द्रीर् सरकार  द्वारा गरठत की  िाने  वाली  संचालन  सजमजत इन जनर्मों के  अधीन ग्रीन क्र े जडट \nकार्ाक्रम के  कार्ाान्वर्न की  जनगरानी  िेतु उिरिार्ी  िोगी। \n(2) संचालन  सजमजत में मंत्रालर्ों र्ा  जवभागों  के  प्रजतजनजध, पर्ाावरण  क्षेत्र के  जविेषज्ञ, उद्योग संघ और  अन्र् \nप्रासंजगक पणधारक  सजम्मजलत िोंगे जिन्िें क ें द्रीर् सरकार  उजचत समझे। \n(3) संचालन  सजमजत, समर्-समर् पर , इन जनर्मों के  अधीन ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम की  समीक्षा करेगी  और  \nजनम्नजलजखत के  संबंध में क ें द्रीर् सरकार  को  जसफाररिें  करेगी , अथाात्:- \n(क ) ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम में सजम्मजलत दकए  िाने  वाले  कार्ा  और  क्षेत्र के  संबंध में; और  \n(ख) क ें द्रीर् सरकार  द्वारा इसको  संिर्भात दकसी  भी मामले  के  संिभा में। \n9. तकनीकी  सजमजत — (1) क ेंद्रीर् सरकार , प्रिासक की  जसफाररिों  के  आधार  पर  प्रत्र्ेक कार्ा  िेतु तकनीकी  सजमजतर्ों का  \nगठन कर  सक ें गी, िो इन जनर्मों के  अधीन ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम के  कार्ाान्वर्न में प्रिासक की  सिार्ता  करेगी।  \n(2) प्रत्र्ेक तकनीकी  सजमजत के  सिस्ट्र्ों में मंत्रालर्ों एवं जवभागों , संगठनों और  उस कार्ा  से संबंजधत क्षेत्र में ज्ञान और  \nअनुभव रखने  वाले  जविेषज्ञ सजम्मजलत िोंगे;  \n(3) तकनीकी  सजमजत जसफारर िें जवकजसत  करेगी  और  प्रिासक को  प्रेजषत करेगी  जिसमें अन्र् बातों  के  साथ -साथ  \nजनम्नजलजखत सजम्मजलत भी िोगा  – \n(क ) वांजित  पर्ाावरणीर्  पररणाम  प्राप्त करने  िेतु आवश्र्क संसाधन , समान  स्ट्क ेल, क्षेत्र, आकार  एवं अन्र् \nप्रासंजगक मापिंडों  की  समकक्षता के  आधार  पर  ग्रीन क्र े जडट की  एक  इकाई  की  गणना  के  जलए पद्धजत; \n(ख) प्रत्र्ेक कार्ा  के  संबंध में रजिस्ट् रीकरण , सत्र्ापन, मूल्र्ांकन, माप  और  ररपोर्टिंग प्रदक्रर्ा के  जलए तंत्र। \n(4) प्रिासक द्वारा तकनीकी  सजमजत को  जनर्िाि दकसी  भी अन्र् तकनीकी  मामले  पर  र्ि परामिा  िेगी। \n10. ग्रीन क्र े जडट रजि स्ट्री - (1) प्रिासक र्ा  नाजमत  अजभकरण  प्रत्र्ेक ग्रीन क्र े जडट का  रजिस्ट् रीकरण  करने  एवं िारी  करने  िेतु \nग्रीन क्र े जडट रजिस्ट्री की  स्ट्थापना एवं रखरखाव  करेगी।  \n(2) रजिस्ट्री इलेक्रॉजनक  डेटाबेस  िोगी, जिसमें अन्र् बातों  के  साथ -साथ , रजिस्ट् रीकरण  एवं ग्रीन क्र े जडट िारी  करने  से \nसंबंजधत सामान्र् डेटा  तत्व सजम्मजलत िोंगे। \n(3)  रजिस्ट्री जनम्नजलजखत कार्ों  का  जनवािन करेगी , अथाात्: - \n\n[भाग  II—खण् ड 3(ii)] भारत का रािपत्र : असाधारण   5 \n(क ) कार्ों  का  रजिस्ट् रीकरण  और  ग्रीन क्र े जडट िारी  करना ; \n(ख) ग्रीन क्र े जडट िारी  करने  का  सटीक  लेखा -िोखा  सुजनजश्चत करना ; \n(ग) सभी आवश्र्क एवं अजनवार्ा  सुरक्षा प्रोटोकॉल के  साथ  सुरजक्षत डेटाबेस  बनाए  रखना ; \n(घ) प्रिासक द्वारा इसे सौंपा  गर्ा  कोई  अन्र् कार्ा।  \n11. रेडडंग  प्लेटफामा - (1) प्रिासक क े न्द्रीर् सरकार  के  अनुमोिन से (व्यापार मंच) रेडडंग  प्लेटफॉमा की  स्ट्थापना करेगा  एवं \nउसका  रखरखाव  करेगा।  \n(2) रेडडंग  प्लेटफॉमा क े न्द्रीर् सरकार  के  अनुमोिन से प्रिासक द्वारा बनाए  गए दििाजनिेिों  के  अनुसार , ग्रीन क्र े जडट के  \nव्यापार संबंधी कार्ा  करेगा।  \n12. ज्ञान एवं डेटा  मंच - (1) प्रिासक दकए  िा  रिे जवजभन्न कार्ों  के  संबंध में पारिर्िाता  प्रिान करने  और  इन जनर्मों के  \nअधीन क्षेत्रवार प्रगजत की  ररपोटा  करने  िेतु, क े न्द्रीर् सरकार  के  अनुमोिन से, ज्ञान एवं डेटा  मंच जवकजसत  करेगा  एवं उसका  \nरखरखाव  करेगा।  \n(2) ज्ञान एवं डेटा  मंच रजिस्ट्री से उत्पन्न प्रमुख डेटा  डबंिुओं और  अन्र् िानकारी , िैसे क्षेत्रवार उपलजब्धर्ां, सवोिम \nप्रथाएं, क्षमता जनमााण  संबंधी िानकारी  आदि को  संगृिीत कर  सक े गा। \n13. अजभजित अजभकरण  - (1) प्रिासक ऐसी संस्ट्थाओं को  क े न्द्र सरकार  द्वारा अनुमोदित दििाजनिेिों  के  अनुसार  अजभजित \nअजभकरण  के  रूप  में कार्ा  करने  के  जलए संस्ट्थाओं को  नाजमत  करेगा।  \n(2) अजभजित अजभकरण  इन जनर्मों के  अधीन ग्रीन क्र े जडट िारी  करने  िेतु आवेिक  द्वारा दकए  गए कार्ों  के  सत्र्ापन \nके  संबंध में दििाजनिेिों  के  अनुसार  सत्र्ापन करवाएगा  और  प्रिासक को  ररपोटा  प्रस्ट्तुत करेगा ;  \n(3) अजभजित अजभकरण  दििाजनिेिों  के  अनुसार  प्रिासक को  वार्षाक  जववरजणर्ां  प्रस्ट् तुत क रेगा।  \n14. ग्रीन क्र े जडट िेतु मांग  उत्पन्न करना  - (1) इन जनर्मों के  अधीन ग्रीन क्र े जडट कार्ाक्रम में भागीिारी  स्ट्वैजछिक भागीिारी  \nपर  आधाररत  िोगी। \n(2) संचालन  सजमजत िेि में ग्रीन क्र े जडट की  मांग  उत्पन्न करने  के  उपार्ों की  जसफाररि  करेगी।  \n15. लेखापरीक्षक . - (1) प्रिासक , अजभजित अजभकरण , रजिस्ट्री, व्यापार मंच और  ज्ञान एवं डेटा  मंच के  कार्ों  की  प्रत्र्ेक \nतीसरे  जविीर् वषा की  समाजप्त पर  एक  वषा के  भीतर  संचालन  सजमजत की  अनुिंसा  पर  क ें द्रीर् सरकार  द्वारा जनर्ुक्त स्ट्वतंत्र \nलेखा  परीक्षकों द्वारा लेखा  परीक्षा करवाई  िाएगी।  \n(2) उप-जनर्म (1) में जनर्िाि लेखा  परीक्षक अपना  लेखा  परीक्षा प्रजतवेिन प्रिासक को  प्रस्ट्तुत करेगा।  \n(3) प्रिासक लेखा  परीक्षा प्रजतवेिन की  प्राजप्त की  तारीख  से िि मास  के  भीतर  लेखा  परीक्षा प्रजतवेिन पर  की  गई \nकारावाई  ररपोटा  क ें द्रीर् सरकार  को  प्रस्ट्तुत करेगा।  \n[फा . सं.12/24/2023 – एचएसएम (पीटी2)] \nनजमता  प्रसाि, संर्ुक्त सजचव \n \n \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE  \nNOTIFICATION \nNew Delhi, the  12th October, 2023 \nS.O. 4458 (E). —WHEREAS a Green Credit programme is being launched at national level to leverage  a \ncompetitive market-based approach for green credit for incentivising environmental actions of various stakeholders;  \nAND WHEREAS the Government of India has introduced ‘ LiFE'-'Lifestyle for Environment’, as a grass -root, \nmass movement, for protection an d conservation of environment and for other environmental and climate gains, by \nenhancing environmental actions that propagate a healthy and sustainable way of living based on traditions and values \nof conservation and moderation, and for sustainable and environment-friendly development; \n\n6  THE GAZETTE OF INDIA : EXTRAORDINARY    [PART II—SEC. 3(ii)] \nAND WHEREAS an innovative market -based mechanism to incentivise environment positive actions will help \npromote the LiFE movement, which aims at encouraging sustainable lifestyles by driving consumer and community \ntowards behavioural changes that promote environment friendly actions; \nAND WHEREAS the Green Credit programme is independent of the carbon credit under the Carbon Credit \nTrading Scheme, 2023 made under the Energy Conservation Act, 2001 (52 of 2001), an environmental activity \ngenerating green credit may have climate co -benefits, such as reduction or removal of carbon emissions and an \nactivity generating green credit under Green Credit programme may also get carbon credit from the same activity \nunder the said Scheme; \nAND WHEREAS the draft Green Credit Programme Implementation Ru les, 2023 was published in the Gazette \nof India, Extraordinary, Part II, Section 3, Sub -section (ii), vide notification number S.O. 2779(E), dated the 26 th June \n2023 inviting objections and suggestions from all persons;  \n AND WHEREAS all the objections and  suggestions received have been duly considered by the Central \nGovernment; \nNOW, THEREFORE, in exercise of the powers conferred by section 3, section 6 and section 25 of the \nEnvironment (Protection) Act, 1986 (29 of 1986), the Central Government hereby make s the following rules, \nnamely:—  \n1. Short title and commencement.— (1) These rules may be called the Green Credit Rules, 2023. \n(2) They shall come into force on the date of their publication in the Oﬃcial Gazette.  \n2. Objectives of Green Credit programme. — (1) The green credit programme shall incentivise environmental \npositive actions through market -based mechanism and generate green credit, which shall be tradable and  made \navailable for trading on a domestic market platform. \n(2) The green credit will arise  from taking measures by a person of any environment activities referred to in \nsub-rule (2) of rule 4.  \n(3) The green credit programme shall encourage industries, companies and other entities to meet their existing \nobligations or other obligations under an y law for the time being in force, and encourage other persons and entities, to \nundertake voluntary environmental measures referred to in rule 4 by generating or buying green credit:  \nProvided that the green credit generated or procured to fulfil any obliga tion in compliance of any law for the \ntime being in force shall not be tradable.  \n3. Definitions.— (1) In these rules, unless the context otherwise requires,— \n(a) “Administrator” means the Administrator referred to in rule 7; \n(b) “designated agency” means an entity designated as such under rule 13;  \n(c) “Green Credit”  means a singular unit of an incentive provided for a speciﬁed activity, delivering a \npositive impact on the environment; \n(d) “Registry” means the Green Credit Registry established under rule 1 0.  \n(2) Words and expressions used in these rules and not deﬁned herein but deﬁned in the Environment \n(Protection) Act, 1986, shall have the same meaning as assigned to them in the Act.   \n4. Green Credit programme. — (1) Any person or entity may take any mea sure specified under sub -rule (2) for \nthe purposes of protection, preservation, or conservation of the environment.  \n(2)  The measures referred to in sub-rule (1) may include the following activities, namely:— \n(i) tree plantation—to promote activities for increasing the green cover across the country; \n(ii) water management—to promote water conservation, water harvesting and water use eﬃciency or water \nsavings, including treatment and reuse of wastewater; \n(iii) sustainable agriculture—to promote natural and regenerative agricultural practices and land restoration to \nimprove productivity, soil health and nutritional value of food produced; \n(iv) waste management—to promote circularity, sustainable and improved practices for waste management, \nincluding collection, segregation, and environmentally sound management; \n(v) air pollution reduction—to promote measures for reducing air pollution and other pollution abatement \nactivities; \n(vi) mangrove conservation and restoration —to promote measures for conservation and r estoration of \nmangroves; \n\n[भाग  II—खण् ड 3(ii)] भारत का रािपत्र : असाधारण   7 \n(vi) ecomark label development—to encourage manufacturers to obtain ecomark label for their goods and \nservices; \n(vii) sustainable building and infrastructure—to encourage the construction of sustainable buildings and other \ninfrastructure using environment friendly technologies and materials. \n(3) A person or entity desirous of obtaining green credit shall register the activity with the Administrator for any \nactivity referred to in sub-rule (2) undertaken by him for grant of green credit.  \n(4) An application for registration under sub-rule (3) shall be made to the Administrator electronically through a \nwebsite established by the Central Government for that purpose.  \n(5) On receipt of the application under sub-rule (4), the Administrator shall cause the activity to be verified by a \ndesignated agency. \n(6) The designated agency shall, after making such verification and inquiry as it may deem necessary, in accordance \nwith the guideline made in this behalf, submit a report to the Administrat or verifying the activities undertaken by the \napplicant. \n(7) On receipt of the report under sub-rule (6) verifying the activities undertaken by the applicant, the Administrator \nshall grant the applicant a certificate of green credit. \n5. Methodology of gene rating green credit. —(1) The methodology for calculating the green credit in respect of \nany activity undertaken shall be such as may be notified by the Central Government on the recommendation of the \nAdministrator.  \n(2) The calculation of green credit in respect of any activity undertaken shall be based on equivalence of resource \nrequirement, parity of scale, scope, size and other relevant parameters required to achieve the desired environmental \noutcome. \n6. Procedure for generation of green credit .— (1) The  methodology for evaluation and verification of the \nactivities undertaken for the purpose of calculation of green credit shall be such as may be determined by the \nAdministrator.  \n(2) The manner of registration of the activities and the details required for  such registration shall be determined \nby the Administrator.  \n(3) The Administrator shall develop the website for registration of activities, evaluation and verification of \nactivities undertaken and award of green credit in respect of such verified activities, electronically. \n7. Administrator. — (1) The Indian Council of Forestry Research and Education, a Society registered under the \nSocieties Registration Act, 1860 (21 of 1860) vide Registration No.596/1990 -91, dated the 12 th March, 1991 and an \nautonomous body as declared by the then Ministry of Environment and Forests, Government of India vide Resolution \nNo.1-8/89-RT, dated the 22nd June, 1990, shall be the Administrator under these rules.  \n(2)  The Administrator shall be responsible for the effective imple mentation of the Green Credit programme, \nincluding its management and operation under these rules. \n(3) The responsibilities of the Administrator shall include the following, namely:— \n(a) develop guidelines, processes and procedures for the implementation o f the green credit programme \nunder these rules; \n(b) develop methodologies, registration process, guidelines and associated measurement, reporting and \nverification mechanism;   \n(c) establish methodologies and processes for issuance of green credit (includin g issuance of digital \ngreen credit), and equivalence of green credit generated from each identified activity;          \n(d) develop guidelines for the establishment and operation of the Green Credit Registry and trading \nplatform; for self-certification or third-party certification for the registration of an activity for issuance of \ngreen credits and its inspection and verification by designated agency, for empanelment of auditors and \naudit by such auditors;  \n(e) establish or designate the Green Credit Registr y, and trading platform service provider in accordance \nwith the approved guidelines; \n(f) develop guidelines for the green credit programme portal, the knowledge and data platform, and for \nthe fees from the registered entities;  \n(g) develop guidelines for f iling of annual returns and progress reports by designated agency, Registry, \ntrading platform and knowledge and data platform; \n\n8  THE GAZETTE OF INDIA : EXTRAORDINARY    [PART II—SEC. 3(ii)] \n(h) develop guidelines for the market stability mechanism for trading of green credit.  \n(i) regulate matters relating to trading o f green credit certificates and to safeguard interest of sellers and \nbuyers; and take preventive and corrective actions to prevent fraud or mistrust.  \n (4) The Administrator shall seek approval of the Central Government for guidelines and methodologies unde r \nsub-rule (3).  \n8. Steering Committee. — (1) A Steering Committee to be constituted by the Central Government shall be \nresponsible for the monitoring of the implementation of the Green Credit programme under these rules.   \n(2) The Steering Committee shall comprise of representatives from the Ministries or Departments, experts from \nthe field of environment, industry associations and other relevant stakeholders as the Central Government may \nconsider appropriate. \n(3) The Steering Committee shall, from time to time, review the Green Credit programme under these rules and \nmake recommendations to the Central Government in respect of following, namely: — \n(a) activities and sectors to be included in the Green Credit programme; and  \n(b) any matter referred to it by the Central Government. \n9. Technical Committee. — (1) The Central Government, based on the recommendations of the Administrator, \nmay constitute Technical Committees for each activity which shall assist the Administrator in implementation of the \nGreen Credit programme under these rules.  \n(2) Each Technical Committee shall comprise of members from the Ministries and Departments, organisations \nand experts having the knowledge and experience in the field related to the activity; \n(3) Technical Committee shall develo p and make recommendations to the Administrator which will inter alia \ninclude— \n(a) methodology for calculation of one unit of Green Credit, on the basis of equivalence of resource \nrequirement, parity of scale, scope, size and other relevant parameters requ ired to achieve the desired \nenvironmental outcome;  \n(b) mechanism for registration, verification, evaluation, measurement and reporting process in respect of \neach activity. \n(4) The Technical Committee shall advise on any other technical matter referred to it by the Administrator. \n10. Green Credit Registry. — (1) The Administrator or designated agency shall establish and maintain a Green \nCredit Registry for the registration and issuance of each Green Credit.   \n(2) The Registry shall be an electronic database,  which, inter alia, shall contain common data elements relevant \nto the registration and issuance of green credit.  \n(3)  The Registry shall discharge the following functions, namely:— \n(a) registration of activities and issuance of green credit; \n(b) ensure accurate accounting of the issuance of green credit; \n(c) maintain secure database with all required and essential security protocols;  \n(d) any other function assigned to it by the Administrator. \n11. Trading platform. — (1) The Administrator shall establish an d maintain a trading platform with the approval \nof the Central Government.  \n(2) The trading platform shall perform functions regarding the trading of green credit, in accordance with the \nguidelines made by the Administrator with the approval of the Central Government. \n12. Knowledge and data platform. — (1) The Administrator shall develop and maintain a knowledge and data \nplatform, with approval of the Central Government, for providing transparency on various activities being undertaken \nand for reporting sectoral progress under these rules. \n(2) The knowledge and data platform may collate key data points generated from the Registry and other \ninformation, such as sectoral achievements, best practices, information on capacity building, etc.  \n13. Designated agency. — (1) The Administrator shall designate such entities to act as designated agency in \naccordance with the guidelines approved by the Central Government. \n\n[भाग  II—खण् ड 3(ii)] भारत का रािपत्र : असाधारण   9 \n(2) The designated agency shall conduct verification and submit reports to the Administrator in accordan ce \nwith the guidelines as regard to the verification of the activities undertaken by an applicant for issuance of green credit \nunder these rules.; \n(3) The designated agency shall file annual returns to the Administrator in accordance with the guidelines.  \n14. Demand generation for green credit. — (1) The participation to the Green Credit programme under these \nrules shall  be based on voluntary participation. \n(2) The Steering Committee shall recommend measures to generate demand for green credit in the country.  \n15. Auditors. — (1) The activities of the Administrator, designated agency, Registry, trading platform and \nknowledge and data platform shall be audited within a period of one year at the end of every third financial year by \nindependent auditors to be appointed by the Central Government on the recommendation of the Steering Committee.  \n(2) The auditor referred to in sub-rule (1) shall submit its audit report to the Administrator. \n(3) The Administrator shall submit an action taken report on the audit report  to the Central Government within \na period of six months from the date of the receipt of the audit report. \n[F. No.12/24/2023–HSM(pt2)] \nNAMEETA PRASAD, Jt. Secy. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nUploaded by Dte. of Printing at Government of India Press, Ring Road, Mayapuri, New Delhi-110064 \nand Published by the Controller of Publications, Delhi-110054.  \nSARVESH KUMAR \nSRIVASTAVA\nDigitally signed by SARVESH KUMAR \nSRIVASTAVA \nDate: 2023.10.13 18:04:03 +05'30'",
