\n1 Jharkhand Ranchi 53 SER Dangoaposi, Distt West \nSinghbhum \n2 Ranchi 54 Palamau, Distt. Palamau \n3 Uttrakhand Dehradun 55 5th Bn SSB Champawat, Distt. \nChampawat \n4 Uttar Pradesh Lucknow 56 Madhupuri, Distt. Fatehpur \n5 Lucknow 57 Sumerpur, Distt. Hamirpur \n6 \nTamilnadu \nChennai 58 ITBP, Idiyapatti, Distt. Madurai \n7 Chennai 59 ITBP ShivgangaiIllupaikudi, Distt. \nShivgangai \n8 Madhya Pradesh Bhopal 60 IIT Indore \n9 Arunachal \nPradesh Tinsukia 61 KV Hayuliang Distt. Anjaw \n10 Odisha Bhubaneswar 62 Chhatarpur, Distt. Ganjam \n2021-22 \n1 Karnataka Bengaluru 63 Sadalgah, Distt, Belagavi \n2 Punjab Chandigarh 64 IIT Ropar, Distt. Roopnagar \n3 Haryana Gurugram 65 Bilaspur,District Yamuna Nagar \n2022-23 \n1 Tripura Silchar 66 Gokulnagar, Distt-Sepahaijala \n2 Odisha Bhubaneswar 67 MCL Subhadra Area Distt- Angul \n3 Odisha Bhubaneswar 68 MCL Jaggannath Area Distt- Angul \n4 Odisha Bhubaneswar 69 IIT Bhubneswar, Distt.-Khordha \n5 Himachal \nPradesh Gurugram 70 Dharampur Distt. Mandi \n2023-24 \n1 Gujarat Ahmedabad 71 Deen Dayal Port Authority Gandhi \nDham, Gujarat \n2 Arunchal Pradesh Tinsukia 72 NIT Arunachal Pradesh, Jote Distt. \nPapum Pare \n3 Punjab Chandigarh 73 Central University of Punjab, Ghudda, \nDistt Bhatinda \n \n*****\n\n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_30": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \n STARRED QUESTION NO. 30 \n  ANSWERED ON 05/12/2023 \n \nREDUCTION IN ACTIVE WORKERS UNDER MGNREGS \n \n*30.  SHRI MANICKAM TAGORE B.: \n \nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether it is a fact that there is a reduction in the number of \nactive workers under the Mahatma Gandhi National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (MGNREGS) from 15.49 crore \nin the previous fiscal year to 14.33 crore as per the data \navailable till 06 October, 2023; \n(b)  whether it is also a fact that while the MGNREGS workforce is \nshrinking, there is an increased demand for work under the \nScheme and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)  whether a net deletion of 80 lakh workers in the ongoing \nfinancial year paints a bleak picture for the MGNREGS and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to identify the factors \ncontributing to this decline and formulate strategies for \nrevitalizing the programme’s participation; and \n(e)  if so, the steps taken in this regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n \n(a) to (e):       A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n   \n  \n  \n \n  \n\nStatement in reply to Parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha  Starred Question \nNo. 30 for 05.12.2023. \n  \n(a): Currently, the beneficiary who has worked during the last \nthree financial years is called an active beneficiary. Mahatma \nGandhi NREGA is a demand driven scheme. If an adult member of a \nhousehold, of the rural area, does not demand for the work during \nthe last three previous financial years, that member will not be in \nthe list of active beneficiary. The change in number of active \nbeneficiary is a continuous process. Currently, there are a total of \n14.37 crore active beneficiaries under Mahatma Gandhi NREGA. \n(b): Mahatma Gandhi NREGA is a demand driven scheme. A total of \n221.60 crore persondays has been generated so far (as on \n30.11.2023), which is more than the persondays generated during \nthe same period in the last financial year i.e. 208.74 crore \npersondays (as on 30.11.2022). \n(c)to(e): Updation/deletion of Job Cards is a regular \nexercise conducted by the States/UTs. The reasons of deletion of \nJob card are listed below: \n(i)      fake job card (incorrect job card),  \n(ii)     duplicate job card,  \n(iii)    not willing to work,  \n(iv)    family shifted from Gram Panchayat permanently and  \n(v)     single person in job card and that person is expired. \n  \n******* \n  \n   \n  \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_31": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 31 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04TH DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nFUNDS UNDER PRADHAN MANTRI ROJGAR PROTSAHAN YOJANA \n \n31.   SHRI MADDILA GURUMOORTHY: \n        SHRIMATI CHINTA ANURADHA: \n      \n       Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of budgetary allocation and release of funds under \nPradhan Mantri Rojgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY) for the State of \nAndhra Pradesh since 2016; \n(b) the details of financial progress made under the scheme in the State \nof Andhra Pradesh since 2016; \n(c) the details of physical targets set and achieved during the above \nperiod; and \n(d) the reasons for not achieving the targets? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI RAMESWAR TELI) \n(a) to (d): Under the Pradhan Mantri Rozgar Protsahan Yojana (PMRPY) \nscheme, funds are disbursed based on Electronic Challan cum Return \n(ECR) filed by the establishment with Employees’ Provident Fund \nOrganisation (EPFO) and the subsidy gets reflected by way of deduction in \nthe total amount payable by the establishment at the time of generation \nof challan. The whole process is online and hence, there is no separate \nfund allocation made state -wise. The details of the total funds released \nand utilized under PMRPY Scheme throughout India and subsidy disbursed \nin respect of the State of Andhra Pradesh since inception of the scheme \ntill date of closure of the scheme (31.03.2022) is as follows: - \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n  \n  \nFinancial Year \nAll India Andhra Pradesh \nFunds \nAllocated/ \nreleased  \n(Rs. in Cr) \nSubsidy \ndisbursed \n(Rs. in Cr) \nSubsidy disbursed \n(Rs. in Cr) \n2016-17 167.69 2.70 \n \n0.05 \n2017-18 470.25 551.28 10.89 \n2018-19 3493.88 4044.10 90.47 \n2019-20 3400 3184.11 85.33 \n2020-21 1255.15 1197.83 32.26 \n2021-22 250 296.35 9.10 \n               Source: EPFO, MoLE \n \nThe scheme was estimated to benefit 20 lakh beneficiaries. The details of \nestablishments and employees benefitted under the scheme throughout India \nand in the State of Andhra Pradesh is as under- \n \n No. of  Beneficiary \nEstablishments \nNo. of \nBeneficiary \nAll India 152900 12169960 \nAndhra \nPradesh \n3397 254891 \n                 Source: EPFO, MoLE \n \n \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_32": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \nLOK SABHA UNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 32 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nWITHDRAWAL OF Rs. 2000 DENOMINATION NOTES \n \n32. SHRI MANISH TEWARI: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the effectiveness of two rounds of demonetization in achieving a “less cash society”, \nconsidering the current substantial rise in cash circulation to Rs. 32.01 lakh crore, a 78 per cent \nincrease since November 4, 2016; \n(b) the rationale behind Reserve Bank of India / Government’s decision to withdraw Rs. 2,000 \ndenomination notes in May 2023 given that the earlier Demonetization of November 2016 had \nhopelessly failed to meet its stated objectives; and \n(c) considering the rise in cash in the system, the manner in which the Government responds to \nthe perception that demonetization failed to create a sustained shift towards digital payments, \nespecially when the currency with the public has risen by 251 per  cent since the withdrawal of \nRs. 500 and Rs. 1,000 notes? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE  \n(SHRI PANKAJ CHAUDHARY) \n \n(a) to (c): The objectives of withdrawal of legal tender character of specified bank notes as stated \nin the Gazette notification S.O. 3407 (E) dated November 08, 2016, issued by Government of \nIndia , are as under:- \n(i) to contain the rising incidence of fake currency notes of the specified banknotes which \nwere largely in circulation, \n(ii) to limit the usage of high denomination banknotes for storage of unaccounted wealth, and \n(iii) to contain the rising level of using fake currency for financing subversive activities like \ndrug trafficking and terrorism.  \n \nThe growth in Notes in Circulation (NiC ) depends on various factors such as expected macro-\neconomic factors like growth in Gross Domestic Product (GDP), inflation, interest rates, growth \nin non-cash modes of payment, etc. As per R eserve Bank of India (R BI), ratio of N iC as on \n\nNovember 4, 2016 to GDP (at current prices) for FY 2015-16 was 12.88% and ratio of NiC as on \nNovember 10, 2023 and to GDP (at current prices) for FY 2022- 23 was 12.22% which are of \nsimilar order. \n \nThe withdrawal of ₹ 2000 denomination banknotes from circulation, announced vide RBI’s Press \nRelease dated 19.05.2023, was a currency management operation and was planned keeping in \nview the Clean Note  Policy of RBI.  About 89% of ₹2000 denomination notes were issued prior \nto March 2017 and are close to the end of their useful life which spans from 4- 5 years. Further, \nthe stock of banknotes in other denominations continues to be adequate to meet the currency \nrequirement of the public.  \n \nThe mission of the Government is to move towards a less cash economy to reduce generation and \ncirculation of black money and to promote digital economy. Digital payments have significantly \nincreased in recent years, as a result of coordinated efforts of the Government with all \nstakeholders. Citizens of the country have access to numerous easy and convenient digital \npayment modes such as Bharat Interface for Money - Unified Payments Interface (BHIM-UPI), \ndebit/ credit cards, Immediate Payment Service ( IMPS), National Electronic Funds Transfer \n(NEFT), Real Time Gross Settlement ( RTGS), Aadhaar Enabled Payment System ( AEPS), \nNational Electronic Toll Collection (NETC) , Prepaid Payment Instruments (PPIs)   which have \nhelped in transforming the digital payment ecosystem increasing person-to-person and person-to-\nmerchant payments.  \n \nThe total digital payment transactions volume increased from 2,071 crore in FY 2017- 18 to \n13,462 crore in FY 2022-23 at a Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR) of 45 per cent.  \n \n******\n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_33": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *33 \nANSWERED ON-05-12-2023 \n  \nSETTING UP OF 6TH STATE FINANCE COMMISSION \n*33. DR. SHASHI THAROOR:  \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is aware that as of March 2023, only nine States have set up the \n6th State Finance Commission (SFC), of which only two are active, according to a report by \nthe Parliamentary Standing Committee on Rural Development and Panchayati Raj; \n(b) whether the Government is aware that few States have not even set up the 4th and 5th SFCs;  \n(c) whether the Government has taken any steps in this regard; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER \n  \nTHE MINISTER OF PANCHAYATI RAJ \n(SHRI GIRIRAJ SINGH) \n  \n(a) to (d) : A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n STATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *33 FOR ANSWER ON 05.12.2023 REGARDING \n‘SETTING UP OF 6TH STATE FINANCE COMMISSION’ \n(a) Yes Sir. It may be mentioned that a   State Finance Commission is active when its \nrecommendations are under finalization. Once the report/ recommendations are submitted its \nfunction is completed. The constitut ion of a   State Finance Commission lies within the \ncompetence and jurisdiction of State Governments. \n(b) Yes Sir. Currently, in Twenty One States, State Finance Commissions have been \nconstituted/have submitted reports.  \n(c) & (d) The Fifteenth Finance Com mission (XV FC) in Chapter 7 (Empowering Local \nGovernments) of its report has recommended that after March 2024, no grants should be \nreleased to a State that has not complied with the constitutional provisions in respect of the \nSFC. Subsequently, Departmen t of Expenditure, Ministry of Finance vide Operational \nGuidelines dated 14-7-2021 has prescribed that the States which have not yet constituted  State \nFinance Commission, must do so and act upon their recommendations and lay the Explanatory \nMemorandum as to the action taken thereon before the State Legislature on or before March, \n2024.  Ministry of Panchayati Raj has shared these guidelines with the States and has engaged \nwith States through meetings/conferences and correspondence on the subject of constitu ting \nthe SFC within the available timeframe and urged the individual States to provide them with \nnecessary logistics support in terms of adequate staff, office space etc. for their effective \nfunctioning. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_34": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No.34 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nInternal Complaints Committee  \n \n*34. SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the Government is aware that sixteen out of thirty sports \nfederations in the country do not have Internal Complaints Committee \n(ICC) as mandated by the 2013 Prevention of Sexu al Harassment \n(PoSH) Act;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof along with the name of such federations \nand the time since when these federations are working without an \nICC;  \n \n(c) whether  the sports federations which do have an ICC, have the \nstipulated number of members, the mandatory external member and \nthe independent member; and  \n \n(d) if so, the details thereof along with the action taken by the \nGovernment in this regard? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n{SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR} \n \n \n(a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 34 FOR REPLY ON 05.12.2023 \nASKED BY SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKADAN  REGARDING INTERNAL \nCOMPLAINTS COMMITTEE. \n \n(a) to (d): Provisions of the Sexual Harassment of Women at \nWorkplace (Prevention,  Prohibition and Redressal) (POSH) Act, 2013 \nare applicable to National Sports Federations (NSFs), as applicable to \nany entity defined in the Act. The NSFs are bound to take action in \nterms of the extant legal provisions in the cases of sexual \nharassment reported to them. \n \nThe Ministry has again written in January 2023 to the Indian Olympic \nAssociation (IOA) and the NSFs to re -examine their structure and \npolicies in terms of the said Act and make the necessary \nchanges/modifications. The Ministry has also asked the IOA and the \nNSFs to sensitize their office bearers, coaches, administrative staff, \nand players about the prevention of sexual harassment of  women in \nsports. \n \nThe Internal Complaints Committee (ICC) has already bee n \nconstituted in the respective NSFs, details of which are given at \nAnnexure. \n \n     ****** \n  \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE RE FERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) to (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.34 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n05/12/2023 ASKED BY  SHRI SHRI THOMAS CHAZHIKA DAN \nINTERNAL COMPLAINTS COMMITTEE. \n \n1. All India Tennis Association \n2. Indian Pancak Silat Federation \n3. Yogasana Bharat \n4. Squash Rackets Federation of India \n5. Archery Association of India \n6. Sepaktakraw Federation of India \n7. Amateur Soft Tennis Federation of India \n8. KIFI Association  \n(Kudo International Federation of India) \n9. Wushu Association of India \n10. Indian Weightlifting Federation \n11. Rowing Federation of India \n12. Athletics Federation of India \n13. Cycling Federation of India \n14. Amateur Kabaddi Federation of India \n15. Kho Kho Federation of India \n16. Indian Golf Union \n17. Gymnastics Federation of India \n18. Paralympic Committee of India \n19. Equestrian Federation of India \n20. Boxing Federation of India \n21. Hockey India \n22. Cerebral Palsy Sports Federation of India \n23. All India Football Federation of India \n24. School Games Federation of India \n25. Ju-Jitsu Association \n26. All India Chess Federation \n27. WAKO India Kickboxing Federation \n28. National Rifle Association of India \n29. Shooting Ball Federation of India \n30. Roller Skating federation of India \n31. Swimming Federation of India \n32. Basketball Federation of India \n33. Fencing Association of India \n34. All India Sports Council of Deaf \n35. Special Olympic Bharat \n36. Bridge Federation of India \n37. Billiards & Snooker Federation of India \n38. Netball Federation of india \n39. Badminton Association of India \n40. Tenpin Bowling Federation (India) \n41. Yachting Association of India \n\n42. Indian Kayaking & Canoeing Association \n43. Indian Polo Association \n44. Indian Triathlon Federation \n45. Table Tennis Federation of India \n46. Roll Ball Federation of India \n47. Atya Patya Federation of India \n48. Tennis Ball Cricket Federation of India \n49. Indian Rugby Football Union \n50. Cycle Polo Federation of India \n51. Softball Federation of India \n52. Taekwondo Federation of India \n53. Wrestling Federation of India* \n* At present, the affairs of Wrestling Federation of India are \nbeing managed by the Ad hoc Committee appointed by IOA. \n \n      *** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_35": "                                                      GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 35 \n \nANSWERED ON MONDAY, 04th DECEMBER, 2023/ AGRAHAYANA 13, 1945 (SAKA) \n \nMECHANISM FOR INTERNATIONAL TRADE SETTLEMENT IN RUPEES \n \n \n35.  SHRI P.V. MIDHUN REDDY:   \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:  \n \n(a) Whether the Reserve Bank of India has recently introduced a new mechanism for \ninternational trade settlements in rupees, aiming to promot e exports and facilitate \nimports; \n(b) if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the expected benefits to be realised due to the same; and \n(d) the details of other such measures, the Government is taking to enhance rupee based \ntrade to reduce its reliance on the US Dollars? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n \n(DR. BHAGWAT KARAD) \n \n(a) to (d): To promote growth of global trade with focus on exports from India, Reserve Bank \nof India (RBI) has put in place an additional arrangement for invoicing, payments, and  \nsettlement of exports/imports in Indian Rupee vide A.P (DIR Series) Circular No. 10 \nRBI/2022-���������������� ������������ ���������������������� ������ ������������������������������ ������������ ���������������������� ������ �������������� ��������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������rom \nvarious countries viz., Bangladesh, Belarus, Botswana, Fiji, Germany, Guyana, Israel, \nKazakhstan, Kenya, Malaysia, Maldives, Mauritius, Myanmar, New Zealand, Oman, Russia, \nSeychelles, Singapore, Sri Lanka, Tanzania, Uganda, United Arab Emirates  (UAE) and \nUnited Kingdom under this arrangement. Besides the aforementioned additional \narrangement, RBI has signed a Memorandum of Understanding (MoU) with Central Bank of \nUAE on 15.07.2023, to promote the use of local currencies. \n \nThe Directorate General of Foreign Tr ade (DGFT) had also introduced Para 2.52(d) in the \nForeign Trade Policy vide Notification No. 33/2015 -20 dated 16.09.2022, to allow for \nInternational Trade Settlement in Indian Rupees (INR) i.e., invoicing, payment, and \nsettlement of exports / imports in I����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\nNo.10 dated 11.07.2022. \n \nChanges were also introduced in the Foreign Trade Policy vide DGFT Notification 43/2015 -\n20 dated 09.11.2022 and Public Notice 35/2015 -20 dated 09.11.2022 for grant of expor ts \nbenefits and fulfilment of Export Obligation for export realisations in Indian Rupees as per the \nRBI guidelines issued under A.P. (DIR Series) Circular No.10 dated 11.07.2022.  \n \nAs the transactions will be settled in lNR, it will reduce the exchange rat e risk for the Indian \nexporters and importers. Further, the increased usage of this additional arrangement may \nalso reduce dependence on freely convertible currencies. \n. \n*** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_36": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. 36 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nRehabilitation of the Villages in Maharashtra \n \n36. SHRI SHRINIWAS PATIL: \n  \nWill the Minister of ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND CLIMATE CHANGE be pleased to \nstate: \n \n(a) whether the Government has received proposals from the State Government of \nMaharashtra for rehabilitation of the villages to convert forest lands;  \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the steps taken/ being taken by the Government in this \nregard;  \n(c) whether the Government has received proposals for widening the forest roads in Satara \ndistrict of Maharashtra;  \n(d) if so, the details thereof, and the steps taken/ being taken by the Government in this \nregard;  \n(e) the current status of the rehabilitation of villages in Koyna Wildlife Sanctuary and \nChandoli National Park in Satara, Sangli, Kolhapur and Ratnagiri districts of \nMaharashtra along with the funds sanctioned for this purpose; and  \n(f) whether all villages have been rehabilitated and if so, the details thereof, district-wise? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT, FOREST AND \nCLIMATE CHANGE  \n(SHRI ASHWINI KUMAR CHOUBEY) \n \n(a) & (b)  The Central Government gets proposals from various States/UTs for conversion \nof forest villages into revenue villages and voluntary village relocation under the \nprovisions of the Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980 from time to time. \n \nThe Government of India vide letter F. No. 8-34/2017-FC dated 20th May 2019 \nhas issued Guidelines for relocation of villages in accordance with the directions \nof the Hon’ble Supreme Court in order dated 28th January, 2019, regarding \nrelocation/rehabilitation of the villages from the core/critical Tiger reserves and \ncritical habitats of the Protected Areas (National Parks and Wildlife Sanctuaries) \nto the periphery of Reserved Forest/Sanctuaries/National Parks subject to certain \nconditions. The proposals are dealt as per rules as and when required.  \n \nAs per the information available the details of the proposals involving relocation \nof villages from Protected Areas (PAs) which have been accorded ‘in-principle’ \napproval by the Central Government under Section 2 (ii) of the Forest \n(Conservation) Act, 1980 in case of Maharashtra as on date is enclosed at \nAnnexure.  \n\n(c) & (d) No proposal has been received by the Central Government for widening the forest \nroads in Satara district of Maharashtra. \n(e) & (f) In case any Project Affected Person (PAP) is required to be relocated from the \nforest area proposed to be diverted under Forest (Conservation) Act, 1980, the \nsame is done by the State Government as per the Rehabilitation & Resettlement \n(R&R) plan prepared by the concerned State Government. Further, the \ninformation regarding such displaced villages and fund sanctioned is not \nmaintained at the Ministry level. \n \n***** \n  \n\nAnnexure \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (a & b) of the Lok Sabha Un-Starred Question No. \n36 due for reply on 04.12.2023 by Shri Shriniwas Patil regarding ‘Rehabilitation of the \nvillages in Maharashtra” \n \nList of the proposals accorded ‘in-principle’ approval by the Central Government for \nForest Village Conversion and Rehabilitation in respect of the Maharashtra  \nSl \nNo. \nFile No. Subject Category Area (in \nha.) \nDate of Stage-I  \napprovals \n1 8-81/2013-FC Distribution for \nagriculture \npurpose to \nPAP's from 7 \nvillages from \nChandoli \nWildlife \nSanctuary in \ndis. Kolhapur \nForest Village \nConversion \n240.62 26/11/2019 \n2 8-31/2019-FC Rehabilitation \nof Rantalodhi \nVillage \nRehabilitation 374.75 02/06/2022 \n3 8-07/2022-FC Rehabilitation \nof Village Vele \nfrom Koyana \nWildlife \nSanctuary i.e. \ncore area of \nSahyadri Tiger \nReserve. \nRehabilitation 112.25 06/03/2023 \n4 8-11/2022-FC Proposal for \nDiversion of \nForest land of \nVillage - \nBahule, Tal. \nPatan, Kharade, \nHelgaon, \nHanbarwadi, \nGosavewadi, \nTal. Karad, \nDist. Satara for \nrehabilitation of \nMale village \nfrom Chandoli \nNational Park \nRehabilitation 227.9 06/03/2023 \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_37": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF EDUCATION \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.37 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nKENDRIYA VIDYALAYAS RUNNING IN \nRENTED ACCOMMODATIONS IN BIHAR \n \n37.  DR. ALOK KUMAR SUMAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of EDUCATION be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  whether it is true that almost all the Kendriya Vidyalayas in Bihar are running in \nrented accommodations despite repeated requests to the Government for \nconstruction of permanent buildings;  \n(b)  if so, the total number of requests received from the Members of Parliament in this \nregard;  \n(c)  whether the Government is planning to build permanent building for Kendriya \nVidyalaya in district Gopalganj; and  \n(d)  if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF EDUCATION \n(SMT. ANNPURNA DEVI) \n \n(a) & (b) As per information received from Kendriya Vidyalaya Sangathan (KVS) no \nKendriya Vidyalayas (KVs) is running in rented accommodations in the State of Bihar. \nHowever, KVs have been made functional in the temporary accommodation provided by \nthe sponsoring authorities till the construction of permanent school building by KVS on the \nland provided by the concerned sponsoring authority . Out of 45 KVs functioning in the \nState of Bihar , 16 KVs are running in temporary building provided by the concerned \nsponsoring authority. Out of these 16 KVs, 03 KVs are at construction stage , land has \nbeen transferred in the name of KVS by the sponsoring authority for KV Banka  and \nconstruction of school buildings for 12 KVs including KV Gopalganj could not be started \nas the sponsoring authorities have not provided requisite extent of land to KVS. Recently, \n3 requests have been received from Member s of Parliament to construct permanent \nbuildings for the KVs that are running in temporary accommodation in the state of Bihar.  \n(c) & (d) Construction of permanent buildings for KVs is a continuous process, which \ndepends upon identification of suitable land, completion of transfer / lease formalities in \nfavour of KVS by the sponsoring authorities, submission of drawings / estimates by \nconstruction agency, availability of funds and requisite approvals etc.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_38": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION No. 38 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.12.2023 \n \nEngagement of Astrologer by AIFF for Team Selection \n \n*38. SHRI S. VENKATESAN: \n \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate : \n \n(a) whether it has come to the notice of the Government the case of \nengagement of Astrologer by All India Football Federation (AIFF) for \nselection of team members; \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c) whether such acts do not diminish the image of the country; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to advise or has advised AIFF \nnot to have parameters other than fitness and ability of the players; \nand \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n{SHRI ANURAG SINGH THAKUR} \n \n \n(a) to (e) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (e) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 38 FOR REPLY ON 05.12.2023 \nASKED BY SHRI S. VENKATESAN  REGARDING ENGAGEMENT OF \nASTROLOGER BY AIFF FOR TEAM SELECTION. \n \n(a) to (e): Sir, the issue of engagement of astrologer by  the All India \nFootball Federation ( AIFF) for the selection of national team members, i f \nany, is a part of the forensic audit report that has been independently \nprepared in terms of the orders of the Hon’ble Supreme Court  in SLP No. \n3047-48 of 2022 titled as “All India Football Federation vs. Rahul Mehra \nand Others. \n \nThe said report has been placed for the consideration in a sealed cover  \nbefore the Hon’ble Supreme Court  by the learned Amicus Curiae in the \nmatter. The matter is still sub judice before the Hon’ble Supreme Court. \n \n****** \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_39": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 39 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 5th DECEMBER, 2023 \n \nNATIONAL MISSION ON SOIL HEALTH CARD \n \n*39.  SHRI RAVNEET SINGH BITTU: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE क ृ िष और िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी \nbe pleased to state: \n(a)  the total number of Soil Health Cards issued by the Government under the National \nMission on Soil Health Card till date, State/UT-wise especially in the State of Punjab; \n(b)  whether adequate measures are being taken by the Government to facilitate soil \ntesting at the district and block levels; \n(c)    if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(d)  whether the Government has a robust monitoring mechanism to ensure regular testing \nof soil and providing information to farmers about proper usage of fertilizers; and  \n(e)   if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nक ृ िष और िकसान कʞ ाण मंũी    (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n(a) to (e):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT IN RESPECT OF PARTS (a) TO (e) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 39 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 5th DECEMBER, 2023 REGARDING \nNATIONAL MISSION ON SOIL HEALTH CARD. \n \n(a): Soil Health Card/ Soil Health Management Schemes under National Project on \nManagement of Soil Health & Fertility are implemented through the State Government since \n2014-15. Now, this scheme has been merged in Rashtriya Krishi Vikas Yojana as Soil \nHealth & Fertility component from year 2022-23. Under the scheme, since, 2014-15, so far \n23.59 crore soil health cards have been issued across the country including Punjab. So far \nas Punjab is concerned, 26.20 lakh soil health cards have been issued in the state.The \nState-wisedetails of number of Soil Health Cards issued under the scheme, including \nPunjab, are at Annexure-I. \n(b) & (c) : The Government is taking adequate measures to facilitate soil testing at the \ndistrict and block levels. Under the scheme, financial assistance of Rs. 75 lakh per lab is \nprovided for setting up static and mobile soil testing labs. There is also provision to provide \nRs. 1.50 lakh for setting up Village Level Soil Testing Labs (VLSTLs) at village / Gram \nPanchayat level.Since, 2014-15, total 8272 Soil Testing Labs (1068 Static, 163 Mobile, \n6376 Mini labs and 665 Village Level labs) have been established in States. In addition, \n1076 Mini Soil Testing labs have also been established in Krishi Vigyan Kendras and other \ninstitutes of Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR). Further to involve other \nstakeholders and to facilitate soil testing at block and village level close to the farmers filed, \nVillage Level Soil Testing Labs (VLSTLs) are set up by entrepreneurs, Self Help Groups \n(SHGs), Farm Producing Organizations (FPO), Primary Agricultural Credit Societies \n(PACS) etc.  \n(d) & (e):  Government is monitoring the scheme implementation through Soil Health \nCard portal (https://www.soilhealth.dac.gov.in ), which is updated on real time basis. A \nmobile app is used which capture geo-tagging to develop a national soil fertility map on a \nGeographic Information System platform. The Soil Health Card Portal has been revamped \nfor uploading test reports directly on portal. The soil health card generated directly \naccessed by farmers through mobile app and sms. Soil testing laboratories are mapped on \nportal with geo-coordinates.   \n\nProgress of the scheme against the target set in Annual Action Plan is reviewed \nregularly through video conferences, physical meetings, State visits etc. \nCrop based fertilizer usage advisories as per soil health cards are issued to farmers \nthrough State Governments, Krishi Vigyan Kendras (KVKs) and Agriculture Technology \nManagement Agencies (ATMAs) Network from time to time.  The Demonstrations on \nbalanced use of fertilizers, based on soil health card recommendations and training to the \nfarmers on proper & integrated use of fertilizers are integral part of the scheme. Indian \nCouncil of Agricultural Research (ICAR) also imparts training, organizes front line \ndemonstrations to educate farmers on these aspects. The Rural Awareness Works \nExperience (RAWE) programme is restructured for Agriculture graduates to generate \nawareness among farmers on use of soil health card for addressing soil health. \nSince 2015, around 6.45 lakh demonstrations, 93781 farmer’s trainings and 7425 \nfarmers melas have been organized through the State Governments under the Scheme.   \n \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nState-wise details of number of Soil Health Cards issued under the scheme \nSl. No. States/UTs Total \n1 Andaman & Nicobar  26,397  \n2 Andhra Pradesh 1,46,31,164  \n3 Arunachal Pradesh  70,273  \n4 Assam  26,64,958  \n5 Bihar  1,46,69,629  \n6 Chhattisgarh 94,71,122  \n7 Dadra & Nagar Haveli and Daman \n& Diu \n1,57,97 \n8 Goa  54,015  \n9 Gujarat  1,39,32,091  \n10 Haryana  84,15,715  \n11 Himachal Pradesh 13,71,653  \n12 Jammu & Kashmir 21,16,492  \n13 Ladakh 1,718  \n14 Jharkhand 9,25,359  \n15 Karnataka  1,70,18,659  \n16 Kerala 33,26,999  \n17 Madhya Pradesh  1,82,44,638  \n18 Maharashtra 2,64,57,060  \n19 Manipur 2,56,864  \n20 Meghalaya  4,37,946  \n21 Mizoram 30,616  \n22 Nagaland 2,27,988  \n23 Odisha  52,00,023  \n24 Puducherry 34,255  \n25 Punjab  26,19,539  \n26 Rajasthan  1,92,57,558  \n27 Sikkim  1,15,085  \n28 Tamil Nadu  1,41,68,507  \n29 Telangana 1,08,39,437  \n30 Tripura  3,20,558  \n31 Uttar Pradesh  3,77,25,218  \n32 Uttarakhand 16,79,779  \n33 West Bengal  95,54,921  \nTotal \n \n23,58,82,033 \n \n \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_4": "������������������� �\n��������������������� �\n������������������������������ �\n��� ������ �\n��������������������� ���\n����������� ������������\n�\n����������������������������� �\n�\n���������������������� �\n������������������������ �\n�������������������� �\n��������������� �������������� �\n�\n����������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ���� ������� ��� ����� ���� ��� ��� ��� ������ ���� ������� �������� ��� ���� �������� ������ ���� ���������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n�\n������ �\n�\n���������������������������������������������� �\n�\n������������������ ��\n�\n���� ����������������� ��� ������� ������������ ������������� �������� ���������� ���������������������� ������\n�������������� ��������� ���� ���������������������������������������� �������������� ������ ������� ��� �������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n���� ������ ������� ������������ ����������� ��� ��������� ����������� ������� ��� ���� ������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������� ����������� �������������� ���� ������������������������������� ��� ����� ����������� ����� �����\n������\n�\n���� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������������������������������\n�\n������\n�\n�",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_40": "������������������� �\n����������������������������� �\n�\n��������� �\n�� ���������������������� �� �\n�������������������� �� ����������� ���� � �\n�������������������������� �\n�\n������������������ � ���� �\n�\n�������� �\n�� ������������������� �\n������������������������������������ �\n�\n��������������������������������������� ������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� �\n�\n������������ �����������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������� ��\n�\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������� ������������� ���������������� ���� �������� ��������� �������� ������ ����\n����� ����� ��\n�\n������������ ����������� ��������������������������������������������������������\n������� ����� �\n�\n���������������� ��������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������ ��\n������ �\n�\n��������� ��� ������ ������������� �������� ��� ���� ��������� ��� ����������� ����\n���������� ���������������� ��������� ��� ������ ������������� �������� ��� ����\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n�\n�������������� ���� ������������ ���������������� ������������ ���� ������ �������� (�������� ��������)������������ ������������ ���� ������ �������� (�������� ��������) \n���� �������������� ������������ ���� ������������ ���� ������ �����������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� �\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� ������������ ���������\n�\n�������������������������������������� � �����������������������������������������\n��� ����� ���������� ����������� ���� ����������� ��� ��������� ���� ��������� ������\n��������� ��������� ��� ��������� �� ��������� ������ ������ ��������� ���� �� �� ����\n������������ ����������� ����������� ������ �