    "ls|LS17_S14_U_64": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA  \nMINISTRY OF JAL SHAKTI \nDEPARTMENT OF WATER RESOURCES, RIVER DEVELOPMENT & GANGA REJUVENATION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *64 \nANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 \nNEW PROJECTS UNDER PMKSY \n*64   SHRI MANNE SRINIVAS REDDY  SHRI KOMATI REDDY VENKAT REDDY \nWill the Minister of JAL SHAKTI be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government has approved the inclusion of new projects under the Pradhan Mantri Krishi \nSinchayee Yojana (PMKSY) – Accelerated Irrigation B enefit Programme (AIBP) which envisages the \nconstruction of new water reservoirs at villages ac ross various rivers including in Andhra Pradesh and  \nTelangana; \n(b) if so, the details thereof State-wise along wit h the present status of implementation and the fund s \nsanctioned under such identified projects; and \n(c) the manner in which it has benefited the farmer s till now and the future action plan thereof along  \nwith funds spent, State-wise? \nANSWER  \nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI  \n(SHRI GAJENDRA SINGH SHEKHAWAT)  \n(a) to (c) : A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\n  \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF STARRED QUESTION \nNO. *64 TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 IN LOK SABHA REGARDING “NEW PROJECTS \nUNDER PMKSY”  \n(a) to (c) \n Yes Sirs. PradhanMantriKrishiSinchayeeYojana (PMKSY ) was launched during the year \n2015-16, with an aim to enhance physical access of water on farm and expand cultivable area under \nassured irrigation, improve on-farm water use effic iency, introduce sustainable water conservation \npractices, etc. Accelerated Irrigation Benefit Programme (AIBP) is one of the components of PMKSY.  \n  \nAt the inception of AIBP under PMKSY in 2016, financial assistance under the scheme was restricted to \nthe identified 99 priority major and medium irrigat ion projects and 7 phases. However, while approving  \ncontinuation of PMKSY-AIBP for the period 2021-2026 , provision for inclusion of other major, \nmedium irrigation projects under PMKSY-AIBP, includ ing extension-renovation-modernization (ERM) \nprojects, has also been approved.  \n  \nThe eligibility criteria for inclusion under the PM KSY-AIBP, inter-alia, require the projects to have \ninvestment clearance by this Ministry. Further, the  project should also be in advanced stage of \nconstruction, having at least 50% overall physical progress, except for either extension- renovation- \nmodernization projects, or projects having command in certain special category areas. The detailed \neligibility criteria and procedure required to be f ollowed for inclusion of such additional projects, \nincluding those on rivers in Andhra Pradesh and Tel angana, have been laid down in detail in the \nguidelines of PMKSY-AIBP which is available at the website of the Ministry.   \n  \nOut of the 99 priority projects (and seven phases) taken up under PMKSY-AIBP in 2016, 54 projects \nhave been reported complete so far. Out of balance priority projects, 21 projects have progress of mor e \nthan 90% and 13 projects have progress between 80 t o 90%. Under these projects, 25.11 lakh hectare \nadditional irrigation potential has been created during 2016-17 to 2022-23. State wise details of stat us of \nthese projects, fund sanctioned, and irrigation potential created through these projects, including projects \nof Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, are placed at Annexure-I. \n  \nFurther, 8 new major/ medium and ERM projects have been included under PMKSY-AIBP subsequent \nto the approval of continuation of PMKSY-AIBP for the period 2021-2026. Statewise details of status of \nthese projects, fund sanctioned and irrigation pote ntial created through these projects, are placed at  \nAnnexure-II .  \n*** \n  \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE-I REFERRED TO IN THE REPLY TO PART (a) TO (c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *64 TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 IN LO K SABHA \nREGARDING “NEW PROJECTS UNDER PMKSY” \n  \nSTATE-WISE STATUS OF ONGOING 99 (AND 7 PHASES) PRIORITY PROJECTS  \n Sl. \nNo. \nState No. of \npriority \nprojects \nCompleted Ongoing Irrigation \nPotential created \nduring 2016-23 \n(thousand hectare) \n \nCentral \nassistance \nreleased during \n2016-23 (Rs. in \ncrore) \n1  Andhra Pradesh 8 1 7 24.33 22.63 \n2  Assam 3 2 1 36.55 0.00 \n3  Bihar 2 0 2 19.64 110.24 \n4  Chhattisgarh 3 2 1 16.76 46.05 \n5  Goa 1 1 0 4.09 0.0 \n6  Gujarat 1 0 1 593.01 4,501.4 \n7  Jharkhand 1 0 1 79.80 756.73 \n8  Karnataka 5 3 2 115.81 1,190.04 \n9  Kerala 2 0 2 2.59 0.00 \n10  Madhya Pradesh 14+7 phases \n=21 17 4 180.20 756.15 \n11  Maharashtra 26 10 16 345.33 2,265.76 \n12 Manipur 2 1 1 15.06 240.10 \n13  Odisha 8 5 3 78.22 1,208.87 \n14  Punjab 2 2 0 34.99 52.42 \n15  Rajasthan 2 2 0 7.24 458.56 \n16  Telangana 11 4 7 187.11 981.48 \n17  Uttar Pradesh 4 1 3 763.93 1,421.82 \n18  UT of J& K 3 3 0 6.52 39.71 \n19  UT of Ladakh 1 0 1 0.00 2.98 \nTOTAL \n106 54 52 2,511.18 14,054.94 \n  \n\n   \nANNEXURE-II  \n  \nANNEXURE-II REFERRED TO IN THE REPLY TO PART (a) TO  (c) OF STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *64 TO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2023 IN LO K SABHA \nREGARDING “NEW PROJECTS UNDER PMKSY” \n  \nSTATUS OF PROJECTS  PMKSY-AIBP PROJECTS INCLUDED DURING 2021-23  \n(TILL DATE)  \nSl. \nNo.  \nState  \nNo. of \nprojects  \nOngoing  \nIrrigation Potential \ncreated (thousand \nhectare)  \nCentral assistance \nreleased during \n2021-23 (Rs. in \ncrore)  \n1  Maharashtra  1 1 7.90  39.02  \n2  Himachal Pradesh  1 1 Nil  2.25  \n3  Rajasthan  1 1 9.14  41.43  \n4  Tamil Nadu  1 1 4.12  34.74  \n5  Assam  1 1 Nil  41.98  \n6  Manipur  1 1 3.71  24.88  \n7  Jharkhand and \nBihar  \n1 1 Nil  Nil  \n8  Uttarakhand  1 1 Nil  Nil  \n  TOTAL  8 8 24.87 184.30 \n \n***  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_65": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 65 \nANSWERED ON 7TH DECEMBER, 2023 \nFATAL ACCIDENTS ON EXPRESSWAYS \n \n*65.  SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI: \nSHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS  \n \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state: \n  \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the  regular fatal accidents \non the expressways have  increased due to heavy commercial \nvehicles parked illegally on road; \n(b)  if so, the steps taken by the Government in this regard; \n(c)  whether Surveillance Vehicles Teams have  been deployed to curb \nsuch menace on the expressways; \n(d)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; \n(e)  whether designated parking lots would be  provided to the heavy \nvehicles on the expressways; and \n(f)  if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n (a) to (f)  A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n\n \n________________________________________________________________________________ \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART  (a) TO ( f) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.  *65 FOR ANSWER  ON 07.12.2023, \nASKED BY SHRI SRIDHAR KOTAGIRI & SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE  \nREGARDING FATAL ACCIDENTS ON EXPRESSWAYS. \n_______________________________________________________________________________________ \n \n(a)     As per data received from Police Departments of States/Union \nTerritories, number of fatal accidents on National Highways (including \nNational Expressways) in the country for the period from 2019 to 2022 is \ngiven in the table below : - \nYear No. of fatal accidents \n2019 51,036 \n2020 45,655 \n2021 50,953 \n2022 55,571 \n  \n(b)  The Ministry, as mandated under enabling provisions of Section 118 \nof the Motor Vehicles Act, 1988 (The MV Act, 1988), notified “The Motor \nVehicles (Driving) Regulations, 2017” vide G.S.R 634 (E) dated 23.06.2017, \nwhich provides  for regulatory requirements to be followed by all road \nusers including drivers of heavy vehicles such as – duties of drivers and \nriders, lane traffic, right of way, stopping and parking, ban on traffic \nimpairment and driving of tractors and goods vehicles . Section 177A of \nThe MV Act, 1988, as stands included vide the Motor Vehicles \n(Amendment) Act, 2019, prescribes penalty for contravention of \nregulations made under section 118. Section 201 of The MV Act, 1988, as \nstands recently amended, provides for penalty for causing obstruction to \nfree flow of traffic by all types of vehicles including heavy vehicles. \n  \nAs per provisions under Section 136A of the Motor Vehicles (Amendment) \nAct, 2019, Ministry has notified rule  for Electronic Monitoring and \nEnforcement of Road Safety vide G.S.R. 575(E) dated 11th August, 2021. \n  \nAdvanced Traffic Management System (ATMS) is presently installed in \nhigh traffic density National Highways and National Expressways such as \nDelhi-Meerut Expressway, Trans-Haryana, Eastern Peripheral Expressway \netc. by National Highways Authority of India (NHAI). \n  \nMinistry has taken various steps in the interest of drivers such as Wayside \nAmenities etc. on National Expressways. \n\n \n  \nMinistry has also circulated guidelines in July 2023 for provision of \nsignages on National Expressways   to provide crucial inform ation to \ndrivers for safe navigation and minimizing risks associated with lane \nchanges. \nToll free Helpline No. 1033 is available 24*7 for assistance of   National \nExpressways' users . \n(c) & (d)      Route Patrolling Vehicles (RPV)/ Highway Surveillance \nVehicles (HSV) deployed by contractors/concessionaires on National \nExpressways have multiple roles including removal of the illegally parked \nvehicles along the National Expressway.   At pre sent, a total of 39 \nHSV/RPV are deployed on the operational National Expressways as \ndetailed below : - \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the National Expressways Operational \nlength  \n(in km) \nNumber of \ndeployed \nHSV/RPV  \n1 Ahmedabad-Vadodara Expressway 93.303 3 \n2 Delhi-Meerut Expressway 84 6 \n3 Eastern Peripheral Expressway 135 5 \n4 Trans-Haryana Expressway 227.02 8 \n5 Delhi-Mumbai Expressway 457.74 17 \n  \n(e) & (f)      The Ministry has issued guidelines dated 11.02.2021 regarding \ndevelopment of Wayside Amenities (WSA) having resting facilities such as \nParking, Dormitory, Dhaba, etc for drivers, for reducing fatigue of long \ndistance travel along the Nationa l Highways and National Expressways. \nNational Highways Logistics Management Limited (NHLML) is developing \nWSA at regular intervals across Brownfield National Highways and \nExpressways.  \n  \nAt present 38 WSA are operational along the National Highways and \nNational Expressways.   Out of the 38 WSA, 20 WSA are along the \noperational National Expressways as detailed at Annexure. \n  \n\n \nAnnexure \n_______________________________________________________________________________________ \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (e) & (f) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *65 FOR ANSWER ON 07.12.2023, ASKED BY SHRI SRIDHAR \nKOTAGIRI & SHRI HEMANT TUKARAM GODSE  REGARDING FATAL \nACCIDENTS ON EXPRESSWAYS. \n \nDetails of 20 Operational WSAs on National Expressways :- \nSl. \nNo State NH/ National \nExpressways Site Description \n1.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK3-9L_Package6_152+500 LHS \n2.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK3-10R_Package6_152+500 RHS \n3.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK3-11L_Package7_191+600 LHS \n4.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK3-12R_Package7_191+600 RHS \n5.  Haryana Delhi-Mumbai DD2-3L_Package3_63+140 LHS \n6.  Haryana Delhi-Mumbai DD2-4R_Package3_69+900 RHS \n7.  Rajasthan Delhi-Mumbai DD4-6L_Package6_172+550 LHS \n8.  Rajasthan Delhi-Mumbai DD4-6R_Package6_172+550 RHS \n9.  Rajasthan Delhi-Mumbai DD3-5R_Package5_125+600 RHS \n10.  Rajasthan Delhi-Mumbai DD3-5L_Package5_125+600 LHS \n11.  Uttar \nPradesh \nEastern \nPeripheral  \nKundli - Palwal/ NE2_39+500_RHS_Milak \nChakarpur_Ghaziabad \n12.  Uttar \nPradesh \nEastern \nPeripheral  \nKundli - Palwal/ NE2_39+500_LHS_Milak \nChakarpur_Ghaziabad \n13.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK2-5L_Package4_93+530 LHS \n14.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK2-6R_Package4_93+530 RHS \n15.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK2-7L_Package5_120+400 LHS \n16.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK2-8R_Package5_120+400 RHS \n17.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK1-1L_Package1_21+100 LHS \n18.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK1-2R_Package1_21+100 RHS \n19.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK1-3L_Package2_50+650 LHS \n20.  Haryana Trans-Haryana AK1-4R_Package2_50+650 RHS \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_66": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 66 \nANSWERED ON 7TH DECEMBER, 2023 \nNHs IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n*66 SHRI SANJAY JADHAV: \n       SHRI VINAYAK RAUT : \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \nसड़क परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \n \nbe pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Government is aware that several National Highways in Maharashtra are in a dilapidated \ncondition;  \n \n(b) if so, the details thereof, district -wise including the present status of minor and major roads under \nParbhani Parliamentary Constituency in particular;  \n \n(c) the details of Centrally sponsored roads in various States including Maharashtra in the country which \nare in a dilapidated condition; \n \n(d) whether the Government makes funds available to the States for maintenance/development of the \nsaid roads through the Central Road Infrastructure Fund (CRIF);  \n \n(e) if so, the details of funds provided to Maharashtra for the said purpose through CRIF since the year \n2014 till date, year-wise;  \n \n(f) the number of roads approved for Maharashtra under CRIF during the last nine years and the present \nstatus thereof;  \n \n(g) the number of roads under CRIF the construction of which have been completed during the said \nperiod; and  \n \n(h) the number of roads, the construction of which is still incomplete including the reasons therefor?  \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n(SHRI NITIN JAIRAM GADKARI) \n \n (a) to (h) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (h) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 66  FOR ANSWER ON 07.12.2023 ASKED BY SHRI SANJAY \nJADHAV AND SHRI VINAYAK RAUT REGARDING NHS IN MAHARASHTRA \n(a) & (b ) The development and maintenance of National Highways (NHs) is a continuous \nprocess and  NHs including those under Parbhani Parliamentary Constituency  in the state of \nMaharashtra are kept in traffic worthy condition and Maintenance/Repair works are taken up as \nrequired. In current Financial Year estimates for length of about 1890 km at an estimated cost of \nRs. 712.83 Crore have been approved for Maintenance and Repair (M&R) of NHs in the State \nof Maharashtra.  \n \n(c) & (d)  The Ministry also allocates funds for State Governments / Union Territories (UTs) for \ndevelopment and maintenance of State Roads under the Central Road & Infrastructure Fund \n(CRIF) and E conomic Importance & Interstate Connectivity (EI&ISC) Schemes as per the \nprovisions of the CRIF Act, 2000  amended by the Finance Act, 2019.  \n The development and maintenance of State roads is the responsibility of respective State \nGovernment/ U.T. Administration. The Ministry gives approval of list of works on State Roads \nproposed by the respective State Government as per accrual under CRIF. The detailed technical, \nfinancial and administrative sanction is granted by respective State/ U.T. Authorities. The \nCentral Government releases funds based on Utilisation Certificate received from the State \nGovernment/ U.Ts. The State Government/ U.T. administration is responsible for contract \nmanagement including quality control of these works.  \n(e)  The details of accrual/allocation of funds and the funds released for development and \nmaintenance of State roads under CRIF scheme for the State of Maharashtra since 2014-15 are \ngiven as below:- \nYear CRIF \nAccrual (Rs. in Crore)  Release (Rs. in Crore) \n2014-15 269.28 100.00 \n2015-16 286.64 429.92 \n2016-17 717.66 709.79 \n2017-18 660.16 795.28 \n2018-19 681.41 780.42 \n2019-20 728.84 629.83 \n2020-21 683.27 675.84 \n2021-22 683.80 390.27 \n2022-23 783.19 1084.15 \n\n2023-24 886.63 749.17 \n   \n(f) to (h) 726 Works in a length of 5458 Km with total Sanctioned cost of Rs. 11531.46 Cr. were \napproved since 2014-15 in the state of Maharashtra. Out of them 341 Works in a length of 3458 \nKm with total cost of Rs. 4990 Cr. were completed and balance works are at im plementation \nstage. The main reasons for delay is due to COVID pandemic, clearances and poor performance \nof contractors.   \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_67": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 67 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON DECEMBER 07, 2023 \n \nIN-SITU SLUM REHABILITATION UNDER PMAY-U \n \nNO.67.  SHRI N. REDDEPPA: \nDR. SANJEEV KUMAR SINGARI: \n \nWill the Minister of HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the details of physical progress of In- situ Slum Rehabilitation (ISSR) \nunder PMAY-U since its inception, State-wise; \n(b) the details of funds allocated and released for In- situ Slum \nRehabilitation under PMAY-U since its inception, State-wise;  \n(c) whether the Government is aware of the unoccupied houses under \nthe ISSR scheme;  \n(d) if so, the steps taken/being taken by the Government to address the \nabove mentioned inoccupancy; and \n(e) the reasons for ISSR having the least number of projects out of all the \nfour verticals in PMAY-U? \n \n         ANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF HOUSING AND URBAN AFFAIRS \n(SHRI HARDEEP SINGH PURI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA S TARRED \nQUESTION NO. 67 * FOR 07.12.2023 REG ARDING ‘IN-SITU SLUM \nREHABILITATION UNDER PMAY-U’ \n \n(a) to (e) : ‘Land’ and ‘Colonisation’ are State subjects. Therefore, \nschemes related to housing including slum dwellers are implemented \nby State/UT Governments in their respective regions. The Ministry of \nHousing and Urban Affairs (MoH UA) is implementing Pradhan Mantri \nAwas Yojana -  Urban (PMAY-U) - 'Housing for All' Mission since June \n25, 2015 to assi st States/  Union Terri tories (UTs) in providing pucca \nhouse with basic amenit ies to all eligible urban beneficiaries a cross \nthe country.  The scheme is implemented through four verticals i.e.  \nBeneficiary Led Construction (BLC), Affordable Housing in Partnership \n(AHP), In -Situ Slum Redevelopment (ISSR) and Credit Linked Subsidy \nScheme (CLSS).  \n \nISSR vertical covers redevelopment of slums, using land as a resource \nfor providing houses to eligible slum dwellers.  Under ISSR, slum \nrehabilitation grant of Rs. 1 lakh per hous e for redevelopment of slums \nis provided. The State/UT wise number of houses sanctioned, grounded \nand completed under ISSR vertical of PMAY-U along with funds \nsanctioned and released is at Annexure. \n \nAs on 20 November 2023 , 2.96 lakh houses have been sanctioned  \nunder ISSR, of which 2.26 lakh have been grounded and 1.56 lakh \nhouses completed and 1.07 lakh occupied. The Ministry  conducts \nrigorous review on monthly basis with States/UTs to expedite \ngrounding & completion and occup ancy of sanctioned projects under \nthe Mission. The States/UTs have been further directed to review the \nprogress of all projects including ISSR vertical for quick addressal \nleading to faster occupancy of completed houses.  \n \nPMAY-U adopts a demand driven approach wherein States/ UTs have \nbeen mandated to carry out demand survey  to cover all eligible \nbeneficiaries through their Urban Loca l Bodies (ULBs)/ implementing \nagencies. The implementing agencies/ beneficiaries are free to choose \nany vertical of the scheme,  depending upon their preferences  and \neligibility criteria . Based on the demand survey  and selection of \nbeneficiaries and local conditions, the project proposals are approved \nby the S tate Level Sanctioning and Monitoring C ommittee (SLSMC) \nheaded by the Chief Secretary of States/UTs for further sanctioning of \nCentral Assistance by Central Sanct ioning and Monitoring Committee \n(CSMC).  \n \n \n\n \n \nMoHUA only considers the release of Central Assistance based on \napproval of projects by State/UT authorities for implementation of the \nproject by them as per their local conditions. It has been observed that \nmajor challenges faced by the implementing agencies include non-\navailability of encumbrance free land, unwillingness of slum dwellers, \ndelay in getting statutory clearances/NoCs, issues pertaining to \nclearing the slums for redevelopment etc.  \n \n***** \n\nAnnexure referred in reply to LS SQ No. 67* due for answer on 07.12.2023 \n \nState/UT-wise number of houses sanctioned, grounded , completed under \nISSR vertical of PMAY-U along with funds sanctioned and released \n \nS. \nNo.   Name of the \nState/UT \nPhysical Progress (Nos.) Financial Progress \n (₹ Crores) \nHouses \nSanctioned \nHouses \nGrounded \nfor \nConstruction \nHouses \nCompleted \nCentral \nAssistance \nSanctioned \nCentral \nAssistance \nReleased  \n1 \nStates \nAndhra Pradesh 1,617 1,617 212 41.26 32.87  \n2 Bihar 6,502 6,502 5,465 179.43 161.70  \n3 Chhattisgarh 300 300 300 6.09 13.54  \n4 Goa - - - - -  \n5 Gujarat 61,794 53,915 34,569 725.80 345.37  \n6 Haryana 2,452 2,085 1,802 153.26 119.28  \n7 Himachal Pradesh 64 64 64 5.89 9.21  \n8 Jharkhand 2,986 2,986 2,846 73.78 73.40  \n9 Karnataka 22,203 22,203 20,980 611.04 540.35  \n10 Kerala 1,183 1,183 731 35.84 37.43  \n11 Madhya Pradesh 7,562 7,562 6,940 214.55 183.92  \n12 Maharashtra 1,45,377 85,605 46,552 1,453.77 -  \n13 Odisha 8,897 8,497 5,416 183.88 170.88  \n14 Punjab 1,025 - - 10.25 7.88  \n15 Rajasthan 14,492 14,492 12,921 239.48 224.54  \n16 Tamil Nadu 4,763 4,763 4,763 132.14 123.70  \n17 Telangana 256 256 256 4.75 62.38  \n18 Uttar Pradesh 5,490 5,490 5,439 190.67 172.07  \n19 Uttarakhand 2,536 2,536 2,270 104.30 107.48  \n20 West Bengal 442 442 192 14.08 11.58  \nSub-total (States) 2,89,941 2,20,498 1,51,718 4,380.28 2,397.57  \n21 \nNorth East States Arunachal \nPradesh 1,536 1,536 1,536 77.39 77.39  \n22 Assam 108 64 - 1.08 -  \n23 Manipur - - - - -  \n24 Meghalaya - - - - -  \n25 Mizoram 142 142 142 9.49 9.30  \n26 Nagaland 1,054 1,054 702 41.68 33.47  \n27 Sikkim - - - - -  \n28 Tripura 3,005 3,005 1,929 77.92 59.94  \nSub- total (N.E. States) 5,845 5,801 4,309 207.56 180.10  \n29 \nUnion Territories A&N Islands - - - - -  \n30 Chandigarh - - - - -  \n31 D&NH and D&D  - - - - -  \n32 Delhi  - - - - -  \n33 Jammu & Kashmir - - - - -  \n34 Ladakh  369 77 62 15.98 11.56  \n35 Lakshadweep - - - - -  \n36 Puducherry  - - - - -  \nSub-total (UT)  369 77 62 15.98 11.56  \nTotal 2,96,155 2,26,376 1,56,089 4,603.82 2,589.24  \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_68": "�������������������  \n�����������������������\n��������������������������������������������������� �������������������\nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *68 \n�����������������������\nFLOODS IN SIKKIM \n���� �����������������������\n������������������������������������������������������\n���� �������� ������ ������� ����� ���� ��� ������������ ������ �������� �������� ����������� ������������ ���� \n���������������������������������������������������������\n�����������������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� ���� �������� ��� ���� ����������� ����� ���� ����������� ����������� ���������� ��� ���� �������� ������ \n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\nANSWER �\nTHE MINISTER OF JAL SHAKTI �\n���������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������\n� �\n\n��������������������������������������������������� �������������������� \n���� ���� ��� ��� ��������� ��� ����������� ��� ���� ������ � ��������� �������� ��� \n�������� �\n��� �������� ������ ������ ������� ��� �������� ���� �������� ��� ���� ������ ������ ��� ���� ����� ���� ���������� ��� ����\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������ \n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n���� ����� ��� ������� ����� ���������� �������� ��� ������������������ ������������ ���� ���������� �������� \n���������������������������\n��� ��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� \n�������������������� \n�� ����������������������������������������������������������������������������������� ",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_69": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF CIVIL AVIATION\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. : 69\n(To be answered on the 7th December 2023)\n \nREGULATORY FRAMEWORK OF AIR SPORTS\n \n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \nWill the Minister of CIVIL AVIATION be pleased to state:-\n \n(a) whether the Government has recently taken any initiative in the field of air\nsports, including any updates or enhancements made to the existing regulatory\nframework and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to bring any special scheme to promote\nexisting air sports hubs in the country including in Narnaul in Haryana and if so,\nthe details thereof;\n(c) the details of countries with which India has signed open sky agreements since\n2017;\n(d) the key accomplishments or progress made in the civil aviation industry as a\nresult of these agreements and the resultant growth in GDP thereof; and\n(e) the other steps being taken by the Government in this regard?\n \n \nANSWER\n \nMinister of CIVIL AVIATION\n \n \n(a) to (e) : A STATEMENT IS LAID ON THE TABLE OF THE HOUSE.\n \n*****\n*69. DR. SUKANTA MAJUMDAR\nSHRI KRIPANATH MALLAH\n be pleased to state:-\n (Shri Jyotiraditya M. Scindia)\n\nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO THE LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 69\nREGARDING REGULATORY FRAMEWORK OF AIR SPORTS DUE FOR\nANSWER ON 07TH DECEMBER 2023\n \n(a) to (b): Yes, Sir. The Ministry of Civil Aviation has introduced National Air\nSports Guidelines (NASG) 2023 to promote air sports culture in the country. \n \nThe guidelines outline key objectives of promoting air sports culture in the country,\nadoption of international good practices related to safe operations, developing an\neffective governance structure, participation in global air sports events and\npromoting manufacturing of air sports equipment in India, etc.\n \nThe key features of regulatory framework are as follows:\n(i)There will be a governance structure for air sports in India through Aero Club of\nIndia which will be the apex body for air sports in India and represent India\nglobally as its National Aerosports Control (NAC).\n(ii)ACI shall provide governance on overall aspects of air sports including but not\nlimited to regulations, certifications, competitions, awards and penalties.\n(iii)There will be an oversight on air sports by Ministry of Civil Aviation through\nAir Sports Federation of India (ASFI).  ASFI will be chaired by Secretary, Ministry\nof Civil Aviation. For any disputes arising between/ among National Commission,\nState Commission, the final authority to settle the dispute will be Air Sports\nFederation of India (ASFI).\n(iv)ACI - (NAC India) in collaboration with DGCA and /or BCAS for each Air\nSports Commission shall lay down the safety, security, standards and maintenance\nfor equipment, infrastructure, personnel and training etc. as per the global best\npractices for its air sport. It shall specify the disciplinary actions to be taken in case\nof deviations and willful noncompliance.\nIndia has high potential for air sports due to its large geographic expanse, diverse\ntopography and different climatic zones.\nThe NASG 2023 creates the enabling provisions for promoting air sports hubs in\nthe country including Narnaul, Haryana.\n \n(c) to (e): Since 2017, India has entered into open sky agreement with 12 countries\nnamely Botswana, Japan, Malta, Morocco, Netherlands, Switzerland, Tanzania,\nZambia, Australia, Maldives, Canada and New Zealand.\nOpen Sky Agreements enhances the air connectivity. They helps the designated\ncarriers of both the countries to mount unlimited operations subject to the\navailability of slots at respective airports.\nFurther, airlines are free to select whatever markets and network they wish to\nservice and operate within the ambit of bilateral agreements. The start of\ninternational flights from any point in India is purely a commercial decision of the\nairlines on the basis of economic viability of the route and other associated factors\nand the government does not interfere in the operational plans of the airlines.\nNo specific details regarding resultant growth in GDP due to Open Sky Agreement\nis available.\n*****",
    "ls|LS17_S14_U_7": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.7 \nANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 \n \nONGOING PROJECTS TO PROMOTE PILGRIM TOURISM \n \n7. SHRI T.N. PRATHAPAN: \n  \n Will the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the tourism Ministry i s contemplating to launch \npilgrim tourism circuits in various parts of the country and if \nso, the details thereof; \n(b) the number of ongoing projects which are currently in place to \npromote pilgrim tourism in the country;  \n(c) the number of pilgrims travel ing across States for religious \njourneys; \n(d) whether the Government is planning to establish a pilgrim \ntourism circuit that connects significant religious sites such \nas the Sreekrishna Temple in Guruvayur, Vadakkumnathan \nTemple in Thrissur, Palayur Church , Koodalmanikyam Temple \nin Irinjalakkuda, Bhagavati Temple in Kodungallur, Cheraman \nJuma Masjid in Kodungallur, Sreeramaswamy Temple in \nThriprayar, etc.; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \n \nTHE MINISTER OF TOURISM          (SHRI G. KISHAN REDDY) \n \n \n(a) to (e): Ministry of Tourism, under its  schemes viz. ‘SWADESH \nDARSHAN’, ‘PRASHAD’ (Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and Spiritual, \nHeritage Augmentation Drive) and ‘Assistance to Central Agencies’ \nprovides financial assistance to the State Governments and Uni on \nTerritories Administrations (UTA) for development of infrastructure \nat tourism destinations including pilgrimage sites in the country. \nThe projects under the schemes are identified in consultation with \nthe State/UT Administration/ Central Agencies and a re subject to \nadherence to the scheme's guidelines, availability of funds and \nstatus of ongoing projects. \n \n\nA total of 76 projects have been sanctioned under ‘SWADESH \nDARSHAN’, 46 projects under ‘PRASHAD’ and 38 projects under \n‘Assistance to Central Agencie s’ Schemes respectively by Ministry \nof Tourism. \n \nThe Ministry does not maintain data on pilgrims travelling \nacross state for religious journeys. However, details of State -wise \nDomestic Tourist Visits (DTVs) and Foreign Tourist Visits (FTVs) \nduring 2018-2022 are annexed at Annexure-I. \n \nThe details of Projects sanctioned by the Ministry of Tourism \nunder the above -mentioned schemes in the State of Kerala are \nannexed at Annexure-II. \n********* \n\nANNEXURE-I \n \n STATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART  (c) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED Q UESTION \nNO.7 ANSWERED ON 04.12.2023 REGARDING ONGOING PROJ ECTS TO \nPROMOTE PILGRIM TOURISM BY SHRI T.N. PRATHAPAN. \n \nDetails of State -wise Domestic Tourist Visits (DTVs) and Foreign Tourist Visits \n(FTVs) during 2018-2022 \n(in Lakhs) \nStates/UTs 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 \nDTV FTV DTV FTV DTV FTV DTV FTV DTV FTV \nAndaman & \nNicobar \nIslands \n4.98 0.15 5.05 0.16 1.91 0.05 1.26 0.02 2.35 0.04 \nAndhra \nPradesh \n1947.68 2.81 2370.52 2.80 708.29 0.68 932.78 0.28 1927.17 1.66 \nArunachal \nPradesh \n5.12 0.08 5.56 0.08 0.43 0.01 1.03 0.00 2.22 0.01 \nAssam 47.11 0.16 54.48 0.27 12.67 0.07 14.09 0.01 83.82 0.09 \nBihar 336.22 10.88 339.90 10.93 56.38 3.08 25.01 0.01 253.30 0.87 \nChandigarh 15.39 0.40 15.64 0.44 4.18 0.12 2.29 0.05 30.27 0.28 \nChhattisgarh 193.30 0.14 173.05 0.07 28.10 0.02 47.47 0.00 236.36 0.00 \nUT of Dadra \n& Nagar \nHaveli & \nDaman & Diu \n15.08 0.07 15.16 0.07 4.02 0.02 6.61 0.00 8.00 0.02 \nDelhi 291.14 27.41 364.68 29.83 95.84 6.81 106.42 1.00 271.86 8.16 \nGoa 70.82 9.34 71.27 9.37 32.59 3.03 33.08 0.22 70.12 1.75 \nGujarat 543.70 5.13 588.65 5.96 194.65 2.10 245.25 0.11 1358.11 17.77 \nHaryana 48.89 0.74 45.49 0.48 21.15 0.17 20.25 0.05 21.08 0.02 \nHimachal \nPradesh \n160.94 3.57 168.29 3.83 31.71 0.43 56.32 0.05 150.71 0.29 \nJammu & \nKashmir \n170.76 1.40 161.63 0.58 25.20 0.05 113.15 0.02 184.99 0.20 \nJharkhand 354.09 1.76 355.81 1.76 25.75 0.00 33.84 0.02 382.84 1.92 \nKarnataka 2143.06 5.44 2279.35 6.09 774.53 1.65 813.34 0.72 1824.13 1.29 \nKerala 156.05 10.96 